\label{l.diffusion.comparison}
Let $\nu_1,\nu_2\in\mathcal P([0,T))$ have finite support, with susceptibilities $A_1,A_2$ and magnetization processes $M^1,M^2$, and let $0\leqslant s\leqslant v\leqslant T$. We recall that $\mathbb E_z$ denotes the expectation when the process starts from $z\in[-1,1]$, the starting time $s$ being understood.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For every continuous convex function $\varphi$ on $[-1,1]$, the map $z\mapsto\mathbb E_z[\varphi(M^1_v)]$ is convex on $[-1,1]$.
\item If $\zeta_{\nu_1}\leqslant\zeta_{\nu_2}$ on $[0,T]$, then $A_1\leqslant A_2$ on $[0,T]\times[-1,1]$.
\item If $A_1\leqslant A_2$, then for every continuous convex function $\varphi$ on $[-1,1]$,
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E_z[\varphi(M_v^1)]\leqslant\mathbb E_z[\varphi(M_v^2)],
 \qquad z\in[-1,1].
\end{equation*}
\end{enumerate}

\noindent\emph{Step 0: regularity.}\par
We drop the index $1$ in this step and the next. In items~(1) and~(3), we may assume that $\varphi$ is smooth on $[-1,1]$, by extending $\varphi$ outside $[-1,1]$ (e.g.\ via a dilation) and mollifying, which preserves convexity and gives uniform approximations; and the case $s=v$ is trivial, so let $s<v$. For $s'\in[s,v]$ and $x\in\mathbb R$, let $F(s',x):=\mathbb E[\varphi(\partial_x\Phi(v,X^{s',x}_v))]$. On each strip, the drift of \eqref{e.scalar.diffusion} is smooth in $x$ with bounded derivatives, so by the parabolic theory used in Lemma~\ref{l.wellposed} and It\^o's formula, $F$ is smooth in $x$ with bounded derivatives on each strip, continuous on $[s,v]\times\mathbb R$, and solves the backward Kolmogorov equation $-\partial_{s'}F=\partial_x^2F+2\zeta\,\partial_x\Phi\,\partial_xF$ classically on each strip. For $|z|<1$, we have $u(s',z):=\mathbb E_z[\varphi(M_v)]=F(s',x(s',z))$, which is therefore smooth on each strip $[v_i,v_{i+1}]\times(-1,1)$ intersected with $[s,v]\times(-1,1)$, with derivatives bounded on $[-1+\eta,1-\eta]$ for every $\eta>0$; the flow of \eqref{e.scalar.diffusion} and the inverse $x(\cdot,\cdot)$ are jointly continuous, so $u$ is continuous on $[s,v]\times(-1,1)$, and on $[s,v]\times[-1,1]$ by \eqref{e.lipschitz.in.z}. Writing $F(s',x)=u(s',\partial_x\Phi(s',x))$, the chain rule and \eqref{e.magnetization.pde} transform the equation for $F$ into
\begin{equation}\label{e.dual.heat}
 -\partial_{s'}u=a\,\partial_z^2u,\qquad a:=A^2,
\end{equation}
on each strip, in the interior.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 1: convexity in the starting point.}\par
Let $z\in(-1,1)$, $x:=x(s,z)$, $X:=X^{s,x}$ and $M_r:=\partial_x\Phi(r,X_r)$. Since the noise in \eqref{e.scalar.diffusion} is additive and the drift is smooth in $x$ on each strip, for every Brownian path the map $x'\mapsto X^{s,x'}_r$ is continuously differentiable, with derivative $J_r:=\exp(2\int_s^r\zeta(w)\partial_x^2\Phi(w,X_w)\,\mathrm d w)\in[1,e^{2T}]$ at $x'=x$. Differentiating under the expectation and using $\partial_zu=\partial_xF/\partial_x^2\Phi$, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
 \partial_zu(s,z)=\mathbb E\bigl[\varphi'(M_v)N_v\bigr],
 \qquad N_r:=\frac{\partial_x^2\Phi(r,X_r)J_r}{\partial_x^2\Phi(s,x)},\quad r\in[s,v].
\end{equation*}
By It\^o's formula, the drift of $\partial_x^2\Phi(r,X_r)$ is $-2\zeta(\partial_x^2\Phi)^2$, as in the proof of Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.ito}, while $\mathrm d J_r=2\zeta\,\partial_x^2\Phi(r,X_r)J_r\,\mathrm d r$, so that
\begin{equation*}
 \mathrm d N_r=\sqrt2\,N_r\,\frac{\partial_x^3\Phi}{\partial_x^2\Phi}(r,X_r)\,\mathrm d B_r.
\end{equation*}
Thus $N$ is a positive continuous local martingale, started from $1$ at time $s$ and bounded by $e^{2T}/\partial_x^2\Phi(s,x)$, hence a martingale with $\mathbb E[N_v]=1$. Let $\widetilde\mathbb P:=N_v\cdot\mathbb P$ on the $\sigma$-algebra at time $v$. By Girsanov's theorem, $\widetilde B_r:=B_r-\sqrt2\int_s^r(\partial_x^3\Phi/\partial_x^2\Phi)(w,X_w)\,\mathrm d w$ is a Brownian motion under $\widetilde\mathbb P$ on $[s,v]$, and
\begin{equation*}
 \mathrm d X_r=b(r,X_r)\,\mathrm d r+\sqrt2\,\mathrm d\widetilde B_r,
 \qquad b:=2\zeta\,\partial_x\Phi+2\,\partial_x\log\partial_x^2\Phi,
\end{equation*}
while $\partial_zu(s,z)=\widetilde\mathbb E[\varphi'(\partial_x\Phi(v,X_v))]$. The function $b$ is smooth in $x$ on each strip, since $\partial_x^2\Phi>0$. For a Brownian motion $B'$ and $x'\in\mathbb R$, the equation $\mathrm d Y_r=b(r,Y_r)\,\mathrm d r+\sqrt2\,\mathrm d B'_r$ on $[s,v]$, started from $x'$ at time $s$, is, for every Brownian path, an ordinary differential equation for $Y-\sqrt2B'$ with a locally Lipschitz vector field: it has a unique solution up to its explosion time, which is a measurable functional of the Brownian path, and two solutions driven by the same Brownian motion from $x'<x''$ remain ordered as long as both exist. Under $\widetilde\mathbb P$, the process $X$ is such a solution, driven by $\widetilde B$ and started at $x$, and it does not explode before $v$, since it is defined on $[s,v]$ almost surely under $\mathbb P$, hence under $\widetilde\mathbb P$. By uniqueness in law, the solution $Y^{x}$ started at $x$ and driven by any Brownian motion does not explode before $v$ either, and $\partial_zu(s,z)=\mathbb E[\varphi'(\partial_x\Phi(v,Y^{x}_v))]$. Now let $z<z'$ in $(-1,1)$, so that $x=x(s,z)<x':=x(s,z')$. Driving $Y^{x}$ and $Y^{x'}$ by the same Brownian motion, we get $Y^{x}_v<Y^{x'}_v$, and since $\varphi'$ and $\partial_x\Phi(v,\cdot)$ are increasing, $\partial_zu(s,z)\leqslant\partial_zu(s,z')$. Hence $u(s,\cdot)$ is convex on $(-1,1)$, and on $[-1,1]$ by continuity. This proves item~(1).

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: comparison of the coefficients.}\par
Adding atoms of mass zero, we may assume that $\nu_1$ and $\nu_2$ have the same atoms $v_0<\cdots<v_\ell$, so that $\zeta_1:=\zeta_{\nu_1}$ and $\zeta_2:=\zeta_{\nu_2}$ are constant on the same strips. For $\theta\in[0,1]$, let $\nu_\theta:=(1-\theta)\nu_1+\theta\nu_2$, whose cumulative function is $\zeta_\theta=\zeta_1+\theta\eta$ with $\eta:=\zeta_2-\zeta_1\geqslant0$, and let $\Phi_\theta$, $x_\theta$, $A_\theta$, $W_\theta$ and $M^\theta$ be the corresponding objects. On each strip, by the recursion \eqref{e.def.scalar.recursion}, $\Phi_\theta(v,x)$ is a finite composition of operators $T_{r,a}$, whose parameters $r=\zeta_\theta(v_i)$ are affine in $\theta$, applied to $\log\cosh$. By the smoothness of $(r,a,x)\mapsto T_{r,a}f(x)$ noted after \eqref{e.def.gaussian.operator}, and by differentiation under the expectation, $\Phi_\theta(v,x)$ is smooth in $(\theta,v,x)$ on $[0,1]\times[v_i,v_{i+1}]\times\mathbb R$, with derivatives in $x$ of all orders bounded. By the inverse function theorem, $x_\theta(v,z)$, $A_\theta(v,z)$ and $W_\theta(v,z)$ are then smooth in $(\theta,v,z)$ on $[0,1]\times[v_i,v_{i+1}]\times(-1,1)$. Let $H:=\partial_\theta W_\theta$, which is continuous on $[0,T]\times(-1,1)$ for each $\theta$. Differentiating \eqref{e.dual.pde} in $\theta$, and using $\partial_\theta(1/\partial_z^2W_\theta)=-A_\theta^2\partial_z^2H$, we find that on each strip,
\begin{equation}\label{e.linear.response.pde}
 -\partial_vH=A_\theta^2\,\partial_z^2H-\eta(v)z^2,\qquad H(T,z)=0,
\end{equation}
the terminal condition coming from $W_\theta(T,\cdot)=I$. Moreover, by Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.continuity}, $\sup_x|\Phi_\theta(v,x)-\Phi_{\theta'}(v,x)|\leqslant|\theta-\theta'|\int_v^T\eta$, and the Legendre transform is $1$-Lipschitz for the uniform norm, so
\begin{equation}\label{e.linear.response.bound}
 |H(v,z)|\leqslant\int_v^T\eta,\qquad (v,z)\in[0,T]\times(-1,1).
\end{equation}
Fix $\theta$, and for this step let $s\in[0,T]$ be arbitrary. Let $z\in(-1,1)$, let $M:=M^\theta$ be started at $(s,z)$, and for small $\varepsilon>0$ let $\tau_\varepsilon$ be its exit time from $[-1+\varepsilon,1-\varepsilon]$. On each strip, $H$ is smooth with bounded derivatives on this interval, so It\^o's formula on $[s,T\wedge\tau_\varepsilon]$, together with \eqref{e.magnetization.sde} and \eqref{e.linear.response.pde}, gives
\begin{equation*}
 H(s,z)=\mathbb E\bigl[H(T\wedge\tau_\varepsilon,M_{T\wedge\tau_\varepsilon})\bigr]
 -\mathbb E\int_s^{T\wedge\tau_\varepsilon}\eta(r)M_r^2\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation*}
Since $M$ takes values in $(-1,1)$ and is continuous, $T\wedge\tau_\varepsilon=T$ for every sufficiently small $\varepsilon$, for every realization. Letting $\varepsilon\to0$, by the continuity of $H$, the bound \eqref{e.linear.response.bound} and bounded convergence,
\begin{equation}\label{e.linear.response}
 H(s,z)=-\int_s^T\eta(r)\,\mathbb E_z\bigl[(M^\theta_r)^2\bigr]\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation}
By item~(1) applied to $\nu_\theta$ and $\varphi(z)=z^2$, each function $z\mapsto\mathbb E_z[(M^\theta_r)^2]$ is convex, and $\eta\geqslant0$, so $H(s,\cdot)$ is concave on $(-1,1)$, that is, $\partial_z^2H\leqslant0$. Hence $\partial_\theta A_\theta=\partial_\theta(1/\partial_z^2W_\theta)=-A_\theta^2\partial_z^2H\geqslant0$: the function $\theta\mapsto A_\theta(s,z)$ is increasing on $[0,1]$, which gives $A_1\leqslant A_2$ on $[0,T]\times(-1,1)$. Both vanish at $z=\pm1$. This proves item~(2).

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: comparison of the processes.}\par
Let $u_i(s',z):=\mathbb E_z[\varphi(M^i_v)]$ for the process started at time $s'$ from $z$, and $a_i:=A_i^2$. By Step~0 and item~(1), $u_1$ is continuous on $[s,v]\times[-1,1]$, smooth on each strip in the interior, convex in $z$, and solves $-\partial_{s'}u_1=a_1\partial_z^2u_1$. Let $|z|<1$ and $\varepsilon>0$, and let $\tau_\varepsilon$ be the exit time of $M^2$, started at time $s$ from $z$, from $[-1+\varepsilon,1-\varepsilon]$. By It\^o's formula on $[s,v\wedge\tau_\varepsilon]$ and \eqref{e.magnetization.sde}, the drift of $u_1(r,M^2_r)$ is $(a_2-a_1)\partial_z^2u_1\geqslant0$, so that $u_1(s,z)\leqslant\mathbb E[u_1(v\wedge\tau_\varepsilon,M^2_{v\wedge\tau_\varepsilon})]$. Letting $\varepsilon\to0$ and using the continuity of $u_1$, we obtain $u_1(s,z)\leqslant\mathbb E[u_1(v,M^2_v)]=u_2(s,z)$ by bounded convergence. The case $|z|=1$ is trivial.

\label{p.path.convexity}
For every $m\in[-1,1]$, the function $g_m$ is convex on $\mathcal Q_1$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.lipschitz}
 |g_m(q)-g_m(q')|\leqslant\|q-q'\|_{L^1},\qquad q,q'\in\mathcal Q_1.
\end{equation}
For a finite-step path \eqref{e.step.path} with $0<q_0<\cdots<q_\ell$ and $|m|<1$, its derivative is
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.path.derivative}
 \partial_{q_i}g_m(q)=(r_{i+1}-r_i)\,\mathbb E_m\bigl[M_{q_i}^2\bigr],
 \qquad i\in\{0,\ldots,\ell\}.
\end{equation}
More generally, for every $a\in\mathcal Q_\infty$, the path
\begin{equation}\label{e.def.z.a}
 z_a(r):=\mathbb E\bigl[(M^{\nu_a,m}_{a(r)})^2\bigr]=\chi_{\nu_a,m}'(a(r)),
 \qquad r\in[0,1),
\end{equation}
is increasing with values in $[m^2,1]$, and it supports $g_m$ at $a$: for every $b\in\mathcal Q_\infty$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.supporting}
 g_m(b)\geqslant g_m(a)+\int_0^1z_a(r)\bigl(b(r)-a(r)\bigr)\,\mathrm d r,
\end{equation}
and the integral in \eqref{e.scalar.supporting} is the right derivative of $\theta\mapsto g_m(a+\theta(b-a))$ at $\theta=0$.

\label{c.psi.m}
For every $m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, the function $\psi_m$ is convex and $(\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S})^*$-increasing on $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, and
\begin{equation}\label{e.initial.lipschitz}
 |\psi_m(q)-\psi_m(q')|\leqslant\sum_s\lambda_s\|q_s-q_s'\|_{L^1}
 \leqslant\|q-q'\|_{L^1},\qquad q,q'\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}.
\end{equation}
For every $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$, the path $p:=(\lambda_sz_{q_s})_{s\in\mathscr S}$, where $z_{q_s}$ is the path \eqref{e.def.z.a} for the magnetization $m_s$, belongs to $\mathcal Q_{\infty,\leqslant\lambda}^{\mathscr S}$ and supports $\psi_m$ at $q$:
\begin{equation}\label{e.vector.supporting}
 \psi_m(q')\geqslant\psi_m(q)+\left\langle q'-q,p\right\rangle_{L^2},
 \qquad q'\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}.
\end{equation}
Finally, let $\mathcal I_\lambda(m):=\sum_s\lambda_sI(m_s)$. For every $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$, the function $m\mapsto\psi_m(q)-\mathcal I_\lambda(m)$ is convex on $[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, and
\begin{equation}\label{e.initial.magnetization.lipschitz}
 \bigl|\psi_m(q)-\psi_{m'}(q)-\mathcal I_\lambda(m)+\mathcal I_\lambda(m')\bigr|
 \leqslant2\sum_s\lambda_s\|q_s\|_{L^1}|m_s-m_s'|,
 \qquad m,m'\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S};
\end{equation}
in particular, $m\mapsto\psi_m(q)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$.

\label{l.gluing}
For every $N$, $t\geqslant0$, $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$, and every $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.gluing}
 0\leqslant\min_k\bigl\{\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-\lambda_N\cdot(hm_{N,k})\bigr\}
 -\overline F_N(t,q)
 \leqslant a_N+2\sqrt{a_N\bigl(t\xi(\mathbf1)+\|q\|_{L^1}\bigr)}.
\end{equation}

We first assume that $q$ is regular, and we denote by $\mathscr G_N(q)$ the middle term in~\eqref{e.gluing}. Let
\begin{equation*}
 V_k(r,x):=N\lambda_N\cdot(hm_{N,k})+U^{\Omega_{N,k}}(r,x;g),
 \qquad
 V(r,x):=U^{\Sigma_N}(r,x;g).
\end{equation*}
By Lemma~\ref{l.symmetries}(1), the functions $V_k$ solve the branch equation \eqref{e.branch.pde} for the path $q$ on $[0,1]\times\mathbb R^N$, and so does $V$; by \eqref{e.terminal.gluing}, their terminal data satisfy $V(1,\cdot)=\log\sum_ke^{V_k(1,\cdot)}$. With $X_0=\sqrt{2\operatorname{diag}(q(0))}\,Z$, the identity \eqref{e.free.energy.V} gives
\begin{align*}
 \overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-\lambda_N\cdot(hm_{N,k})&=t\xi(\lambda_N)+\lambda_N\cdot q(1)-\frac1N\mathbb E\,V_k(0,X_0),\\
 \overline F_N(t,q)&=t\xi(\lambda_N)+\lambda_N\cdot q(1)-\frac1N\mathbb E\,V(0,X_0),
\end{align*}
so that
\begin{equation}\label{e.D.N}
 \mathscr G_N(q)=\frac1N\Bigl(\mathbb E\,V(0,X_0)-\max_k\mathbb E\,V_k(0,X_0)\Bigr).
\end{equation}

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 1: a logarithmic sum of solutions.}\par
Fix $\rho\in(0,1]$, and let $W:=\rho^{-1}\log\sum_ke^{\rho V_k}$ and $\pi_k:=e^{\rho V_k}/\sum_je^{\rho V_j}$, so that $\sum_k\pi_k=1$. Then $\partial_rW=\sum_k\pi_k\partial_rV_k$, $\nabla W=\sum_k\pi_k\nabla V_k$, and
\begin{equation*}
 \nabla^2W=\sum_k\pi_k\nabla^2V_k
 +\rho\sum_k\pi_k(\nabla V_k-\nabla W)(\nabla V_k-\nabla W)^{\mathsf T}.
\end{equation*}
Since each $V_k$ solves the branch equation, we have $\mathcal L[V_k]=0$, where $\mathcal L$ is the operator defined in \eqref{e.def.branch.operator}. Using this and the identity $\sum_k\pi_k\nabla V_k\cdot D\nabla V_k-\nabla W\cdot D\nabla W=\sum_k\pi_k(\nabla V_k-\nabla W)\cdot D(\nabla V_k-\nabla W)$, we find
\begin{equation}\label{e.logsum}
 \mathcal L[W]=(\rho-r)\,\mathcal V,
 \qquad
 \mathcal V:=\sum_k\pi_k(\nabla V_k-\nabla W)\cdot D(\nabla V_k-\nabla W).
\end{equation}
The matrix $D(r)$ is diagonal with entries $\dot q_s(r)$ on $I_{N,s}$, and $|\partial_{x_i}V_k|\leqslant1$ for every $i$ and $k$, so that the variance of $\partial_{x_i}V_k$ under the weights $\pi_k$ is at most $1$, and
\begin{equation*}
 0\leqslant\mathcal V\leqslant\operatorname{tr} D(r)=N\lambda_N\cdot\dot q(r).
\end{equation*}
The sign of $\mathcal L[W]$ is favorable only for $r\geqslant\rho$. For $r<\rho$, we compensate it by the function $c(r):=N\int_r^1(\rho-u)_+\,\lambda_N\cdot\dot q(u)\,\mathrm d u$, which satisfies $\mathcal L[W+c]=(\rho-r)\mathcal V-(\rho-r)_+N\lambda_N\cdot\dot q(r)\leqslant0$ on $[0,1)\times\mathbb R^N$. The function $W+c$ is continuous with bounded gradient and classical on each piece of $q$. At $r=1$, we have $W(1,\cdot)=\rho^{-1}\log\sum_ke^{\rho V_k(1,\cdot)}\geqslant\log\sum_ke^{V_k(1,\cdot)}=V(1,\cdot)$, because $p\mapsto(\sum_ka_k^p)^{1/p}$ is decreasing for positive $a_k$ and $\rho\leqslant1$. Lemma~\ref{l.comparison}, applied to $W_1=W+c$ and $W_2=V$, gives $V\leqslant W+c$; and since $V_k(1,\cdot)\leqslant V(1,\cdot)$, it also gives $V_k\leqslant V$ for every $k$. Hence, for every $x\in\mathbb R^N$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.root.comparison}
 \max_kV_k(0,x)\leqslant V(0,x)\leqslant\frac1\rho\log\sum_ke^{\rho V_k(0,x)}+c(0),
\end{equation}
where an integration by parts gives $c(0)=N\bigl(\int_0^\rho\lambda_N\cdot q(u)\,\mathrm d u-\rho\,\lambda_N\cdot q(0)\bigr)$.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: Gaussian concentration at the root.}\par
We evaluate \eqref{e.root.comparison} at $x=X_0$ and take expectations. The lower bound and \eqref{e.D.N} give $\mathscr G_N(q)\geqslant0$. For the upper bound, each $Y_k:=V_k(0,X_0)$ is a function of the standard Gaussian vector $(g,Z)$. By \eqref{e.response.first}, applied to the branch of the slice $\Omega_{N,k}$ started from $(0,x)$, the gradient of $U^{\Omega_{N,k}}(0,x;g)$ in $g$ is $\sqrt{2t}$ times a conditional mean of $v_\sigma$, hence of norm at most $\sqrt{2tN\xi(\lambda_N)}$ by \eqref{e.gram}; and the gradient of $V_k(0,X_0)$ in $Z$ is $\sqrt{2\operatorname{diag}(q(0))}\,\nabla_xV_k$, of squared norm at most $2\sum_sn_{N,s}q_s(0)=2N\lambda_N\cdot q(0)$, since $|\partial_{x_i}V_k|\leqslant1$. Thus $Y_k$ is a Lipschitz function of $(g,Z)$ with constant $\Lambda:=\sqrt{2N(t\xi(\lambda_N)+\lambda_N\cdot q(0))}$, and the Gaussian concentration inequality, in its exponential form $\mathbb E\,e^{\rho(Y_k-\mathbb E Y_k)}\leqslant e^{\rho^2\Lambda^2/2}$, together with Jensen's inequality, gives
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E\,\frac1\rho\log\sum_ke^{\rho Y_k}
 \leqslant\frac1\rho\log\sum_k\mathbb E\,e^{\rho Y_k}
 \leqslant\max_k\mathbb E\,Y_k+\frac{\log M_N}\rho+\rho N\bigl(t\xi(\lambda_N)+\lambda_N\cdot q(0)\bigr).
\end{equation*}
We insert this into \eqref{e.root.comparison} and divide by $N$. The term $\rho\,\lambda_N\cdot q(0)$ cancels against $c(0)/N$, and \eqref{e.D.N} and \eqref{e.def.aN} yield
\begin{equation}\label{e.gluing.rho}
 \mathscr G_N(q)\leqslant\frac{a_N}\rho+\rho\,t\xi(\lambda_N)+\int_0^\rho\lambda_N\cdot q(u)\,\mathrm d u
 \leqslant\frac{a_N}\rho+\rho\bigl(t\xi(\mathbf1)+\|q\|_{L^1}\bigr),
\end{equation}
where we used that $\xi(\lambda_N)\leqslant\xi(\mathbf1)$, and that the average of the increasing function $\lambda_N\cdot q$ over $[0,\rho]$ is at most its average over $[0,1]$, which is $\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}\|q_s\|_{L^1}\leqslant\|q\|_{L^1}$.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: choice of $\rho$, and general paths.}\par
Let $C:=t\xi(\mathbf1)+\|q\|_{L^1}$. If $C\leqslant a_N$, we take $\rho=1$ in \eqref{e.gluing.rho}; otherwise, we take $\rho=\sqrt{a_N/C}$. In both cases, $\mathscr G_N(q)\leqslant a_N+2\sqrt{a_NC}$, which is \eqref{e.gluing} for regular paths. For $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$, let $q^n$ be regular paths with $\|q^n-q\|_{L^1}\to0$, which exist by the density noted in the proof of Proposition~\ref{p.extension}. All the free energies in \eqref{e.gluing} satisfy the Lipschitz bound \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}, and so does their minimum over $k$, so that $|\mathscr G_N(q)-\mathscr G_N(q^n)|\leqslant2\|q^n-q\|_{L^1}$; since $\|q^n\|_{L^1}\to\|q\|_{L^1}$, \eqref{e.gluing} for $q^n$ gives \eqref{e.gluing} for $q$.

\label{l.factorization}
For every $N$, $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$, $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$ and $k$, we have
\begin{align}
 \overline F_N(0,q)&=\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}\,\psi_\circ(q_s;h_s),
                                            \label{e.factorization}\\
 \overline F_{N,k}(0,q)&=\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}\,G_{n_{N,s},k_s}(q_s).
                                            \label{e.slice.factorization}
\end{align}
Moreover, for every $n\geqslant1$ and $a\in\mathcal Q_1$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.single.configuration}
 G_{n,0}(a)=G_{n,n}(a)=\log2+\int_0^1a(r)\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation}

By the Lipschitz bound \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}, by Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.continuity} and by the density of regular paths, it suffices to consider a regular path $q$. At $t=0$, the slice $\Omega_{N,k}$ is the product over $s$ of the one-species slices $\Omega_{n_{N,s},k_s}$ in the coordinates $I_{N,s}$, and the terminal function \eqref{e.def.terminal} is
\begin{equation*}
 U(1,x)=\sum_s\log\sum_{\sigma_s\in\Omega_{n_{N,s},k_s}}
            2^{-n_{N,s}}\exp(x_{I_{N,s}}\cdot\sigma_s),
\end{equation*}
the sum over the blocks $I_{N,s}$ of the terminal functions of the one-species slices. By Lemma~\ref{l.symmetries}(2), $U(0,x)=\sum_sU_s(0,x_{I_{N,s}})$, where $U_s$ is the solution of the branch equation for the one-species slice $\Omega_{n_{N,s},k_s}$ and the path $q_s$; moreover the blocks $X_{0,I_{N,s}}=\sqrt{2q_s(0)}\,Z_{I_{N,s}}$ of the initial field are independent, each with the law of the initial field of the corresponding one-species system. Hence $\mathbb E\,U(0,X_0)$ is the sum over $s$ of the corresponding one-species quantities, and \eqref{e.slice.factorization} follows from \eqref{e.free.energy.V}, since $\lambda_N\cdot q(1)=\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}q_s(1)$ and $N^{-1}=\lambda_{N,s}n_{N,s}^{-1}$.

For the full cube with the weights $2^{-N}e^{h^{(N)}\cdot\sigma}$, we take the sites as blocks. The terminal function is $U^{\Sigma_N}(1,x)=\sum_s\sum_{i\in I_{N,s}}\log\cosh(x_i+h_s)$. By Lemma~\ref{l.symmetries}(1) and (2), $U^{\Sigma_N}(0,x)=\sum_s\sum_{i\in I_{N,s}}u_s(0,x_i+h_s)$, where $u_s$ is the solution of the one-spin branch equation \eqref{e.one.spin.branch} for the path $q_s$, and the coordinates of $X_0$ are independent with $X_{0,i}=\sqrt{2q_s(0)}\,Z_i$ for $i\in I_{N,s}$. By Lemma~\ref{l.dictionary}, $\mathbb E\,u_s(0,h_s+\sqrt{2q_s(0)}\,Z)=q_s(1)-\psi_\circ(q_s;h_s)$, hence
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E\,U^{\Sigma_N}(0,X_0)
 =\sum_sn_{N,s}\bigl[q_s(1)-\psi_\circ(q_s;h_s)\bigr],
\end{equation*}
and \eqref{e.factorization} follows from \eqref{e.free.energy.V}.

Finally, the slice $\Omega_{n,n}$ consists of the single configuration $\mathbf1$, so that the overlap $R_n(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)=\frac1n\sigma^1\cdot\sigma^2$ of two replicas is identically $1$. For a regular path $a$ and $\theta>0$, the path $\theta a$ is regular, and $\theta\mapsto\theta a$ is an admissible variation with $h=a$, so Theorem~\ref{t.derivatives} gives
\begin{equation*}
 \frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm d\theta}G_{n,n}(\theta a)=\int_0^1a(r)\,\mathbb E\langle R_n\rangle_r\,\mathrm d r=\int_0^1a(r)\,\mathrm d r
 \qquad(\theta>0).
\end{equation*}
The function $\theta\mapsto G_{n,n}(\theta a)$ is continuous on $[0,\infty)$ by \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}, and $G_{n,n}(0)=-\frac1n\log2^{-n}=\log2$ by Lemma~\ref{l.zero}. Integrating over $[0,1]$ proves \eqref{e.single.configuration} for $k=n$ and regular $a$; the slice $\Omega_{n,0}=\{-\mathbf1\}$ is treated identically, and the general case follows by \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}.

\label{l.block}
Let $t=0$, let $\mathscr S=\mathscr S_0\cup\mathscr S_1$ be a partition of the species, and let $q=(q^{(0)},0)$, where $q^{(0)}$ is a regular path indexed by $\mathscr S_0$ and $q_s\equiv0$ for $s\in\mathscr S_1$. Let $N_0$ be the number of coordinates of the species in $\mathscr S_0$, write $\sigma_{(0)}$ for the restriction of $\sigma\in\Omega$ to these coordinates, let $\Omega_{(0)}:=\{\sigma_{(0)}:\sigma\in\Omega\}$, and let $w_{(0)}(\tau):=\sum_{\sigma\in\Omega:\sigma_{(0)}=\tau}w(\sigma)$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{e.block}
 N\overline F_{N,\Omega}(0,q)=N_0\overline F_{N_0,\Omega_{(0)}}(0,q^{(0)}),
\end{equation}
where the right-hand side is the free energy of the system with the $N_0$ coordinates of the species in $\mathscr S_0$, the configuration set $\Omega_{(0)}$ and the weights $w_{(0)}$.

For $\varepsilon>0$, let $q^\varepsilon:=(q^{(0)},\varepsilon(1+r)\mathbf1)$, which is regular and converges to $q$ in $L^1$, so that $\overline F_{N,\Omega}(0,q^\varepsilon)\to\overline F_{N,\Omega}(0,q)$ by \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}. Let $N_1:=N-N_0$, write $x=(x_{(0)},x_{(1)})$ according to the two groups of species, let $U^\varepsilon$ be the solution of \eqref{e.branch.pde} for $q^\varepsilon$ in dimension $N$, and recall from \eqref{e.hessian.bounds} that $0\leqslant\operatorname{tr}\nabla^2U^\varepsilon\leqslant N$ and $|\nabla U^\varepsilon|^2\leqslant N$. Let $V$ be the solution of the branch equation in dimension $N_0$, for the path $q^{(0)}$, with the terminal datum $x_{(0)}\mapsto U^\varepsilon(1,x_{(0)},0)=\log\sum_{\tau\in\Omega_{(0)}}w_{(0)}(\tau)e^{x_{(0)}\cdot\tau}$, which is the terminal function of the reduced system. The function $W(r,x_{(0)}):=U^\varepsilon(r,x_{(0)},0)$ solves the equation of the reduced system up to the source $-\varepsilon(\operatorname{tr}\nabla_{(1)}^2U^\varepsilon+r|\nabla_{(1)}U^\varepsilon|^2)(r,x_{(0)},0)$, which is bounded in absolute value by $2\varepsilon N$, so Lemma~\ref{l.comparison} gives $|W-V|\leqslant2\varepsilon N$. Moreover $X^\varepsilon_0=(X_{0,(0)},\sqrt{2\varepsilon}Z_{(1)})$, with $X_{0,(0)}\sim\mathcal N(0,2\operatorname{diag}(q^{(0)}(0)))$ the initial field of the reduced system and $Z_{(1)}$ a standard Gaussian vector in $\mathbb R^{N_1}$, so that \eqref{e.heat.interpolation}, applied in the second group of coordinates, gives $0\leqslant\mathbb E\,U^\varepsilon(0,X^\varepsilon_0;g)-\mathbb E\,W(0,X_{0,(0)})\leqslant N\varepsilon$. By \eqref{e.free.energy.V}, since $N\lambda_{N,s}=n_{N,s}$,
\begin{align*}
 N\overline F_{N,\Omega}(0,q^\varepsilon)&=\sum_{s\in\mathscr S_0}n_{N,s}q_s(1)+2\varepsilon N_1-\mathbb E\,U^\varepsilon(0,X^\varepsilon_0;g),\\
 N_0\overline F_{N_0,\Omega_{(0)}}(0,q^{(0)})&=\sum_{s\in\mathscr S_0}n_{N,s}q_s(1)-\mathbb E\,V(0,X_{0,(0)}),
\end{align*}
and letting $\varepsilon\to0$ proves \eqref{e.block}.

\label{l.composition}
For every $n\geqslant1$, $0\leqslant k,k'\leqslant n$, and $a\in\mathcal Q_1$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.composition}
 \bigl|(G_{n,k}(a)-I_{n,k})-(G_{n,k'}(a)-I_{n,k'})\bigr|
 \leqslant2\|a\|_{L^1}\,|m_k-m_{k'}|,
\end{equation}
where $I_{n,k}:=\log2-\frac1n\log\binom nk$ satisfies $|I_{n,k}-I(m_k)|\leqslant\frac{\log(n+1)}n$.

\label{p.scalar.initial}
For every $R<\infty$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.initial.limit}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\ \sup_{0\leqslant k\leqslant n}\ \sup_{\substack{a\in\mathcal Q_1\\\|a\|_{L^1}\leqslant R}}
 \bigl|G_{n,k}(a)-g_{m_k}(a)\bigr|=0.
\end{equation}
More precisely, with $\varepsilon_n(R):=\log(n+1)/n+2\sqrt{R\log(n+1)/n}$, for every $0\leqslant k\leqslant n$ and every $a\in\mathcal Q_1$ with $\|a\|_{L^1}\leqslant R$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.initial.bound}
 0\leqslant G_{n,k}(a)-g_{m_k}(a)\leqslant E_n(R):=\varepsilon_n(R)+4R\sqrt{2\varepsilon_n(R)}+\frac{2\log(n+1)}n.
\end{equation}

We first assume that $a$ is bounded, so that $\nu_a\in\mathcal P([0,T])$ for some $T>0$ and the results of Section~\ref{s.scalar} apply to it; note that $\int v\,\nu_a(\mathrm d v)=\|a\|_{L^1}\leqslant R$. We write $d_k:=G_{n,k}(a)-g_{m_k}(a)$, and we begin with two preliminary bounds.

Lemma~\ref{l.gluing} at $t=0$, applied to the system with a single species and $n$ sites, for which $a_N$ becomes $\log(n+1)/n$, together with \eqref{e.factorization} for this system, which reads $\overline F_n(0,a)=\psi_\circ(a;h)$, gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.gluing}
 0\leqslant\min_{0\leqslant j\leqslant n}\{G_{n,j}(a)-hm_j\}-\psi_\circ(a;h)\leqslant\varepsilon_n(R)
 \qquad\text{for every }h\in\mathbb R.
\end{equation}
The lower bound gives $G_{n,k}(a)\geqslant\psi_\circ(a;h)+hm_k$ for every $k$ and every $h\in\mathbb R$; taking the supremum over $h$ and recalling the definition \eqref{e.def.g.m} of $g_m$, we obtain $d_k\geqslant0$ for every $k$. Next, by Lemma~\ref{l.composition}, including its entropy estimate, and by \eqref{e.magnetization.lipschitz}, for all $j,k$,
\begin{multline}\label{e.d.continuity}
 |d_k-d_j|\leqslant\bigl|(G_{n,k}(a)-I_{n,k})-(G_{n,j}(a)-I_{n,j})\bigr|
 +\frac{2\log(n+1)}n\\
 +\bigl|(g_{m_k}(a)-I(m_k))-(g_{m_j}(a)-I(m_j))\bigr|
 \leqslant4R|m_k-m_j|+\frac{2\log(n+1)}n.
\end{multline}

By \eqref{e.scalar.duality}, the upper bound in \eqref{e.scalar.gluing} says that the Legendre transforms in the magnetization of $k\mapsto G_{n,k}(a)$ and of $m\mapsto g_m(a)$ nearly coincide. A Legendre transform identifies in general only a convex envelope; here, the strong convexity of $g_m$ in $m$ localizes the minimizing slice. Let $0<k<n$, so that $|m_k|<1$, let $h:=y(\nu_a,m_k)$, so that $\psi_\circ(a;h)=g_{m_k}(a)-hm_k$ by \eqref{e.scalar.duality}, and let $j$ attain the minimum in \eqref{e.scalar.gluing}. The upper bound there reads
\begin{equation*}
 d_j+\bigl[g_{m_j}(a)-g_{m_k}(a)-h(m_j-m_k)\bigr]\leqslant\varepsilon_n(R),
\end{equation*}
and by the strong convexity \eqref{e.strong.convexity} at $m_0=m_k$, where $y(\nu_a,m_k)=h$, the bracket is at least $\frac12(m_j-m_k)^2$. Since $d_j\geqslant0$, we obtain
\begin{equation}\label{e.localization}
 d_j+\tfrac12(m_j-m_k)^2\leqslant\varepsilon_n(R):
\end{equation}
the minimizing slice has both a small error and a magnetization close to $m_k$. Transporting its error to the slice $k$ by \eqref{e.d.continuity},
\begin{equation*}
 d_k\leqslant d_j+4R|m_j-m_k|+\frac{2\log(n+1)}n
 \leqslant\varepsilon_n(R)+4R\sqrt{2\varepsilon_n(R)}+\frac{2\log(n+1)}n=E_n(R).
\end{equation*}
For $k\in\{0,n\}$, we have $m_k=\pm1$, and \eqref{e.single.configuration} and \eqref{e.g.endpoints} give $d_0=d_n=0$. This proves \eqref{e.scalar.initial.bound} for bounded $a$.

Let finally $a\in\mathcal Q_1$ with $\|a\|_{L^1}\leqslant R$, and let $a^M:=a\wedge M$ for $M>0$. Then $a^M$ is bounded, $\|a^M\|_{L^1}\leqslant R$, and $\|a^M-a\|_{L^1}\to0$ as $M\to\infty$. Both $G_{n,k}$ and $g_{m_k}$ are $1$-Lipschitz for the $L^1$ norm, by \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz} and \eqref{e.scalar.lipschitz}, so $d_k$ at $a$ is the limit of $d_k$ at $a^M$, which lies in $[0,E_n(R)]$. Since $E_n(R)\to0$ as $n\to\infty$, this also proves \eqref{e.scalar.initial.limit}.

\label{c.initial.limit}
For every $R<\infty$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.initial.limit}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\ \sup_k\ \sup_{\substack{q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}\\\|q\|_{L^1}\leqslant R}}
 \bigl|\overline F_{N,k}(0,q)-\psi_{m_{N,k}}(q)\bigr|=0.
\end{equation}

\label{t.cx}
Let $\psi:\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}\to\mathbb R$ be $(\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S})^*$-increasing and $1$-Lipschitz for the $L^1$ norm.
\begin{enumerate}
\item There exists a unique Lipschitz viscosity solution $f$ of \eqref{e.hj.H} on $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$ with $f(0,\cdot)=\psi$.
\item If $u$ and $v$ are respectively a Lipschitz viscosity subsolution and a Lipschitz viscosity supersolution of \eqref{e.hj.H} on $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, and $u(0,\cdot)\leqslant v(0,\cdot)$ on $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, then $u\leqslant v$ on $\mathbb R_+\times\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$.
\item If $\psi$ is convex, then for every $(t,q)\in\mathbb R_+\times\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.hopf.general}
 f(t,q)=\sup_{p\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}}\inf_{q'\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}}
 \left\{\psi(q')+\left\langle q-q',p\right\rangle_{L^2}+t\int_0^1\xi(p(r))\,\mathrm d r\right\}.
\end{equation}
\item For every $L$ that is a power of $2$, the equation \eqref{e.hj.H} on $\mathsf C_L$, with initial condition $x\mapsto\psi(\iota_Lx)$, has a unique Lipschitz viscosity solution $f_L$, the comparison principle of item~(2) holds on $\mathsf C_L$, and $f_L(t,\pi_Lq)\to f(t,q)$ as $L\to\infty$, for every $(t,q)\in\mathbb R_+\times\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$.
\end{enumerate}

\label{l.stability}
In either of the two settings above, let $v$ be a Lipschitz viscosity solution of \eqref{e.hj.H}, let $\varepsilon\geqslant0$, and let $u$ be a Lipschitz function on $\mathbb R_+\times C$ such that $u-\varepsilon t$ is a viscosity subsolution and $u+\varepsilon t$ a viscosity supersolution of \eqref{e.hj.H}. Let $\omega:\mathbb R_+\to\mathbb R_+$ be increasing and Lipschitz, and suppose that $|u(0,x)-v(0,x)|\leqslant\omega(\|x\|)$ for every $x\in C$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{e.stability}
 |u(t,x)-v(t,x)|\leqslant\varepsilon t+\omega\bigl(\|x\|+L_\mathsf Ht\bigr)
 \qquad\text{for every }(t,x)\in\mathbb R_+\times C.
\end{equation}

We first assume that $\omega$ is smooth with bounded derivative. For $a>0$, let $n_a(x):=(\|x\|^2+a^2)^{1/2}$, which is smooth with $\|\nabla n_a\|\leqslant1$ and $n_a\geqslant\|x\|$, and let
\begin{equation*}
 \Gamma(t,x):=\omega\bigl(n_a(x)+L_\mathsf Ht\bigr).
\end{equation*}
The function $\Gamma$ is smooth and Lipschitz, and $\partial_t\Gamma=L_\mathsf H\,\omega'\geqslant L_\mathsf H\|\nabla\Gamma\|$, the derivative of $\omega$ being evaluated at $n_a(x)+L_\mathsf Ht$. We claim that $v+\Gamma$ is a Lipschitz viscosity supersolution. Let $\phi$ be smooth and such that $(v+\Gamma)-\phi$ has a local minimum at $(t,x)$. Then $\phi-\Gamma$ is smooth and $v-(\phi-\Gamma)$ has a local minimum at $(t,x)$, so $\partial_t\phi-\partial_t\Gamma-H(\nabla\phi-\nabla\Gamma)\geqslant0$, and the Lipschitz continuity of $H$ gives
\begin{equation*}
 \partial_t\phi-H(\nabla\phi)
 \geqslant\partial_t\Gamma-\bigl|H(\nabla\phi)-H(\nabla\phi-\nabla\Gamma)\bigr|
 \geqslant\partial_t\Gamma-L_\mathsf H\|\nabla\Gamma\|\geqslant0.
\end{equation*}
In the same way, $v-\Gamma$ is a Lipschitz viscosity subsolution. At $t=0$, since $\omega$ is increasing and $n_a\geqslant\|x\|$, the assumption gives $u(0,\cdot)\leqslant v(0,\cdot)+\Gamma(0,\cdot)$ and $v(0,\cdot)-\Gamma(0,\cdot)\leqslant u(0,\cdot)$. The comparison principle of Theorem~\ref{t.cx}, applied to the pairs $(u-\varepsilon t,v+\Gamma)$ and $(v-\Gamma,u+\varepsilon t)$, gives $|u-v|\leqslant\varepsilon t+\Gamma$, and letting $a\to0$ yields \eqref{e.stability}. For a general $\omega$, we extend it by $\omega(0)$ to the negative half-line, and we apply the previous case to $\omega_b(s):=\int\omega(s+b-y)\eta_b(y)\,\mathrm d y$, where $b>0$ and $\eta_b$ is a smooth probability density supported in $[-b,b]$. Each $\omega_b$ is smooth and increasing, with derivative bounded by the Lipschitz constant of $\omega$, it satisfies $\omega_b\geqslant\omega$ since $\omega$ is increasing, and $\omega_b\to\omega$ pointwise as $b\to0$; the conclusion for $\omega_b$ passes to the limit.

\label{l.cone.preservation}
The map $A_L$ sends $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$ into $\mathsf C_L$, and
\begin{equation}\label{e.adjoint}
 \left\langle\mathcal T_Lz,p\right\rangle_{L^2}=\left\langle z,A_Lp\right\rangle_L
 \qquad\text{for all }z\in(\mathbb R^{\mathscr S})^L\text{ and }p\in{L^2}.
\end{equation}

\label{p.approximate}
Fix $N$, a slice $\Omega_{N,k}$, $0<\kappa\leqslant1$, and a power of two $L$. For $x\in\mathsf C_L$, let
\begin{equation}\label{e.shifted.approximation}
 q^\kappa_x(r):=\mathcal T_Lx(r)+\kappa(1+r)\mathbf1,
 \qquad u(t,x):=\overline F_{N,k}(t,q^\kappa_x).
\end{equation}
The function $u$ is Lipschitz on $\mathbb R_+\times\mathsf C_L$, and the functions $u-\varepsilon t$ and $u+\varepsilon t$ are respectively a viscosity subsolution and a viscosity supersolution of \eqref{e.hj.H} on $\mathsf C_L$, where
\begin{equation}\label{e.finite.residual}
 \varepsilon:=15M_\xi(\kappa N)^{-1/3}+\frac{3L_\xi}{L},
 \qquad \text{with } \ L_\xi:=\sup_{[0,1]^{\mathscr S}}|\nabla\xi|,
\end{equation}
and where we recall that $M_\xi=\sup_{[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}\|\nabla^2\xi\|$.

\noindent\emph{Step 1: the residual.}\par
The shift by $\kappa(1+r)\mathbf1$ in \eqref{e.shifted.approximation} has two purposes: it supplies the positive path slope required by Theorem~\ref{t.remainder}, and it makes $u$ differentiable in a neighborhood of the closed cone. Indeed, for $x\in\mathsf C_L$, every coordinate of $q^\kappa_x$ starts at $x_{1,s}+\kappa\geqslant\kappa$ and has slope at least $\kappa$. Moreover, $q^\kappa_x$ remains a regular path, with positive initial value and positive slopes, on the open neighborhood of $\mathsf C_L$ where
\begin{equation*}
 x_{1,s}>-\kappa,\qquad
 x_{j+1,s}-x_{j,s}>-\kappa/L .
\end{equation*}
On this neighborhood, the variations $x\mapsto q^\kappa_x$ are admissible in the sense of Section~\ref{s.derivatives}, and the derivatives of Theorem~\ref{t.derivatives} depend continuously on $(t,x)$ by the parameter dependence noted after Lemma~\ref{l.comparison}. Consequently $u$ is continuously differentiable on this neighborhood for $t>0$, with a right derivative in $t$ at $t=0$. We use the scalar product $\left\langle\cdot,\cdot\right\rangle_L$ to define gradients. By Theorem~\ref{t.derivatives} and \eqref{e.adjoint}, the projected gradient is still an average of expected Gibbs overlaps,
\begin{equation}\label{e.projected.gradient}
 \nabla_Lu=A_Lp_N,\qquad p_N(r)=\mathbb E\langle R_N\rangle_r,
 \qquad
 \partial_tu=\int_0^1\mathbb E\langle\xi(R_N)\rangle_r\,\mathrm d r ,
\end{equation}
where the Gibbs averages are those of the path $q^\kappa_x$. By Lemmas~\ref{l.energy} and \ref{l.cone.preservation}, $A_Lp_N\in\mathsf C_L$; also $0\leqslant(A_Lp_N)_{j,s}\leqslant\lambda_{N,s}$, so that each $(A_Lp_N)_j$ lies in the positive part of the closed unit ball, and $\mathsf H_L(A_Lp_N)=L^{-1}\sum_j\xi((A_Lp_N)_j)$. Since $w_j$ is supported in the cell $J_j$ and its neighbors, the $L^1$ distance between $p_N$ and $\iota_LA_Lp_N$ is at most $3/L$ times the sum over $s$ of the total variations of $p_{N,s}$, which is at most $\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}=1$. This gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.quadrature}
 \left|\mathcal H(p_N)-\mathsf H_L(\nabla_Lu)\right|
 =\left|\int_0^1\xi(p_N(r))\,\mathrm d r-\frac1L\sum_j\xi\bigl((A_Lp_N)_j\bigr)\right|\leqslant\frac{3L_\xi}{L}.
\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{e.defect.identity}, Theorem~\ref{t.remainder} and \eqref{e.quadrature}, we obtain, for every $x\in\mathsf C_L$ and $t>0$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.residual.bound}
 |\partial_tu-\mathsf H_L(\nabla_Lu)|\leqslant\varepsilon .
\end{equation}
The function $u$ is Lipschitz in $x$ by \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz} and \eqref{e.interpolation.error}, since $\|\mathcal T_L(x-y)\|_{L^1}\leqslant\|\mathcal T_L(x-y)\|_{L^2}\leqslant|x-y|_L$, and in $t$ since $|\partial_t\overline F_{N,k}|\leqslant\xi(\mathbf1)$.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: contacts at the boundary.}\par
Suppose that a smooth function $\phi$ is such that $u-\phi$ has a local maximum at $(t,x)\in(0,\infty)\times\mathsf C_L$, relative to the closed cone. The extension of $u$ to an open neighborhood gives $\partial_t\phi=\partial_tu$ and, for every $y\in\mathsf C_L$, the point $x+\theta y$ belongs to $\mathsf C_L$ for $\theta>0$, so $\left\langle\nabla_L\phi-\nabla_Lu,y\right\rangle_L\geqslant0$; that is, $\nabla_L\phi-\nabla_Lu\in\mathsf C_L^*$. Since $\mathsf H_L$ is increasing for the dual-cone order, \eqref{e.residual.bound} gives $\partial_t\phi-\mathsf H_L(\nabla_L\phi)\leqslant\partial_tu-\mathsf H_L(\nabla_Lu)\leqslant\varepsilon$. At a local minimum, the dual-cone relation is reversed and gives $\partial_t\phi-\mathsf H_L(\nabla_L\phi)\geqslant-\varepsilon$. Hence $u-\varepsilon t$ is a viscosity subsolution and $u+\varepsilon t$ a viscosity supersolution of the equation on $\mathsf C_L$.

\label{p.convergence}
For every $N$, every slice $\Omega_{N,k}$, every $t\geqslant0$, every $q\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, every $0<\kappa\leqslant1$, and every $R>\|q\|_{L^2}+L_\mathsf Ht$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.convergence}
 \bigl|\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-f_{m_{N,k}}(t,q)\bigr|
 \leqslant\delta_N(R)+3\kappa+15M_\xi\,t\,(\kappa N)^{-1/3}.
\end{equation}

Write $m:=m_{N,k}$, fix a power of two $L$, let $u$ be the function \eqref{e.shifted.approximation}, and let $f_{m,L}$ be the solution of Theorem~\ref{t.cx}(4) with initial condition $x\mapsto\psi_m(\iota_Lx)$. By \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}, for every $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$ and $t\geqslant0$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.shift.cost}
 \bigl|\overline F_{N,k}\bigl(t,q+\kappa(1+r)\mathbf1\bigr)-\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)\bigr|
 \leqslant\sum_s\lambda_{N,s}\int_0^1\kappa(1+r)\,\mathrm d r=\frac32\kappa ,
\end{equation}
which is the cost of the shift in \eqref{e.shifted.approximation}.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 1: the initial data.}\par
Let $x\in\mathsf C_L$. By \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz}, \eqref{e.shift.cost} and \eqref{e.interpolation.error},
\begin{equation*}
 |u(0,x)-\overline F_{N,k}(0,\iota_Lx)|
 \leqslant\frac32\kappa+\|\mathcal T_Lx-\iota_Lx\|_{L^1}
 \leqslant\frac32\kappa+\frac{\sqrt{|\mathscr S|}}{4\sqrt L}\,|x|_L.
\end{equation*}
If $|x|_L\leqslant R$, then $\|\iota_Lx\|_{L^2}=|x|_L\leqslant R$, and $|\overline F_{N,k}(0,\iota_Lx)-\psi_m(\iota_Lx)|\leqslant\delta_N(R)$ by definition. Otherwise, let $x':=(R/|x|_L)x$, which belongs to $\mathsf C_L$ and satisfies $\|\iota_L(x-x')\|_{L^1}\leqslant\|\iota_L(x-x')\|_{L^2}=|x|_L-R$; since $\overline F_{N,k}(0,\cdot)$ and $\psi_m$ are $1$-Lipschitz for the $L^1$ norm, by Proposition~\ref{p.extension} and \eqref{e.initial.lipschitz}, we get $|\overline F_{N,k}(0,\iota_Lx)-\psi_m(\iota_Lx)|\leqslant\delta_N(R)+2(|x|_L-R)$. Altogether,
\begin{equation}\label{e.initial.error}
 |u(0,x)-\psi_m(\iota_Lx)|\leqslant\omega(|x|_L),
 \qquad
\text{ where } \  \omega(s):=\delta_N(R)+\frac32\kappa+\frac{\sqrt{|\mathscr S|}}{4\sqrt L}\,s+2(s-R)_+ .
\end{equation}

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: comparison.}\par
The function $\omega$ is increasing and Lipschitz. By Proposition~\ref{p.approximate} and Lemma~\ref{l.stability}, applied on $\mathsf C_L$ with $v=f_{m,L}$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.comparison.error}
 |u(t,x)-f_{m,L}(t,x)|\leqslant\varepsilon t+\omega\bigl(|x|_L+L_\mathsf Ht\bigr)
 \qquad\text{for every }(t,x)\in\mathbb R_+\times\mathsf C_L,
\end{equation}
with $\varepsilon$ as in \eqref{e.finite.residual}.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: passage to the limit.}\par
Let $x:=\pi_Lq$. Since $|x|_L\leqslant\|q\|_{L^2}$ and $R>\|q\|_{L^2}+L_\mathsf Ht$, the last term of $\omega$ vanishes in \eqref{e.comparison.error}, which gives
\begin{equation*}
 |u(t,\pi_Lq)-f_{m,L}(t,\pi_Lq)|
 \leqslant\varepsilon t+\delta_N(R)+\frac32\kappa+\frac{\sqrt{|\mathscr S|}}{4\sqrt L}\bigl(\|q\|_{L^2}+L_\mathsf Ht\bigr).
\end{equation*}
By \eqref{e.finite.lipschitz} and \eqref{e.shift.cost}, $|\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-u(t,\pi_Lq)|\leqslant\|q-\mathcal T_L\pi_Lq\|_{L^1}+\frac32\kappa$. We now let $L\to\infty$ along the powers of two: $\|q-\mathcal T_L\pi_Lq\|_{L^1}\to0$, the term $3L_\xi t/L$ in $\varepsilon t$ and the last term of the previous display vanish, and $f_{m,L}(t,\pi_Lq)\to f_m(t,q)$ by Theorem~\ref{t.cx}(4). This proves \eqref{e.convergence}.

Let $t\geqslant0$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, and choose $R>\|q\|_{L^2}+L_\mathsf Ht$. Proposition~\ref{p.convergence} with $\kappa=N^{-1/4}$ gives
\begin{equation*}
 \sup_k\bigl|\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-f_{m_{N,k}}(t,q)\bigr|
 \leqslant\delta_N(R)+(3+15M_\xi t)N^{-1/4}\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}0,
\end{equation*}
which is \eqref{e.slice.convergence}.

It remains to show that $m\mapsto f_m(t,q)$ is continuous. Let $m,m'\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, and let $c:=\mathcal I_\lambda(m)-\mathcal I_\lambda(m')$, with $\mathcal I_\lambda$ as in Corollary~\ref{c.psi.m}. The function $\psi_{m'}+c$ satisfies the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{t.cx}, and its solution is $f_{m'}+c$. By \eqref{e.initial.magnetization.lipschitz}, for every $q'\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$,
\begin{equation*}
 |\psi_m(q')-(\psi_{m'}(q')+c)|
 \leqslant2\sum_s\lambda_s\|q'_s\|_{L^1}|m_s-m'_s|\leqslant2\|q'\|_{L^2}\,|m-m'|_\infty,
\end{equation*}
where $|m-m'|_\infty:=\max_s|m_s-m'_s|$. Lemma~\ref{l.stability} on $\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, with $u=f_m$, $v=f_{m'}+c$, $\varepsilon=0$ and $\omega(s)=2s|m-m'|_\infty$, gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.f.magnetization.lipschitz}
 \bigl|f_m(t,q)-f_{m'}(t,q)-\mathcal I_\lambda(m)+\mathcal I_\lambda(m')\bigr|
 \leqslant2\bigl(\|q\|_{L^2}+L_\mathsf Ht\bigr)|m-m'|_\infty .
\end{equation}
Since $\mathcal I_\lambda$ is continuous on $[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, so is $m\mapsto f_m(t,q)$.

\label{l.path.bidual}
For every $m\in[-1,1]$ and $a\in\mathcal Q_\infty$, the path $z_a$ of \eqref{e.def.z.a} satisfies $J_m(z_a)=\int_0^1z_aa-g_m(a)$, and
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.path.bidual}
 g_m(a)=\sup_{\substack{z\in\mathcal Q_\infty\\0\leqslant z\leqslant1}}
       \left\{\int_0^1z(r)a(r)\,\mathrm d r-J_m(z)\right\},
\end{equation}
the supremum being attained at $z=z_a$.

\label{l.hopf.conjugate}
For every $m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, $t\geqslant0$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.hopf.conjugate}
 f_m(t,q)=\sup_{p\in\mathcal Q_{\infty,\leqslant\lambda}^{\mathscr S}}
 \left\{\left\langle q,p\right\rangle_{{L^2}}+t\int_0^1\xi(p(r))\,\mathrm d r-\psi_m^*(p)\right\}.
\end{equation}
Moreover, for every $p\in\mathcal Q_{\infty,\leqslant\lambda}^{\mathscr S}$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.conjugate.separable}
 \psi_m^*(p)=\sum_s\lambda_s\,J_{m_s}\Bigl(\frac{p_s}{\lambda_s}\Bigr),
\end{equation}
where $J_{m_s}$ is the path conjugate \eqref{e.def.J}.

\label{l.localization}
Let $g(\nu)=\sum_s\lambda_sg_s(\nu_s)$ be a functional of the marginals, as above.
\begin{enumerate}
\item For every $\chi\in\mathfrak X$ and every $x\in\mathbb R_+^{\mathscr S}$ with $x_s+t\partial_s\xi(\lambda)\leqslant T_s$ for all $s$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.localized.evolution}
 \mathsf S_t\chi(x)=\sup_{p\in\Lambda_\lambda}
 \left\{p\cdot x+t\xi(p)
              -\sup_{y\in [0,T]}(p\cdot y-\chi(y))\right\}.
\end{equation}
In particular, $\mathsf S_t\chi(x)$ depends only on the restriction of $\chi$ to $[0,T]$; and if $\chi\leqslant\chi'+c$ on $[0,T]$ for a constant $c$, then $\mathsf S_t\chi(x)\leqslant \mathsf S_t\chi'(x)+c$.
\item Suppose that each $g_s$ is $1$-Lipschitz for the $1$-Wasserstein distance. Then the infimum in \eqref{e.def.support.value} may be restricted to the potentials $\chi=\sum_s\lambda_s\chi_s$ such that $\chi_s$ has slope $1$ on $[T_s,\infty)$ for every $s$, and for these potentials, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.localized.measure.conjugate}
 g_*(\chi)=\inf_{\nu\in\mathcal P([0,T])}
                         \left\{\int\chi\,\mathrm d\nu-g(\nu)\right\}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

\label{l.scalar.bidual}
For every $m\in[-1,1]$, $T>0$ and $\nu\in\mathcal P([0,T])$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.scalar.measure.bidual}
 g_m(\nu)=\inf_{\chi\in\mathfrak X_1}
                         \left\{\int\chi\,\mathrm d\nu-(g_m)_*(\chi)\right\},
\end{equation}
and the infimum is attained at the potential $\widehat\chi_{\nu,m}\in\mathfrak X_1$ that coincides with the supporting potential $\chi_{\nu,m}$ of Corollary~\ref{c.scalar.support} on $[0,T]$ and has slope $1$ on $[T,\infty)$.

\label{l.scalar.conjugacies}
For every $v\in\mathcal V$ and every $\rho\in\mathcal P([0,1])$,
\begin{align}
 J^*(v)&=-g_*(v^*),                                  \label{e.J.star.g}\\
 J(\rho)&=\sup_{v\in\mathcal V}\left\{\int v\,\mathrm d\rho-J^*(v)\right\}.
                                                     \label{e.J.bidual}
\end{align}
In particular, $J^*$ is finite, convex and $1$-Lipschitz for the uniform norm on $\mathcal V$.

\noindent\emph{Step 1: a pointwise conjugacy.}\par
Let $\chi\in\mathfrak X_1$ have slope $1$ beyond $x_1\geqslant0$, and let $z\in\mathcal Q_\infty$ with $0\leqslant z\leqslant1$. We claim that
\begin{equation}\label{e.pointwise.conjugacy}
 \sup_{a\in\mathcal Q_\infty}\int_0^1[z(r)a(r)-\chi(a(r))]\,\mathrm d r
 =\int_0^1\chi^*(z(r))\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation}
The inequality $\leqslant$ is clear. For the converse, let $a(r)$ be the smallest maximizer of $x\mapsto z(r)x-\chi(x)$ on $\mathbb R_+$; it exists and belongs to $[0,x_1]$, since this function is concave and decreasing beyond $x_1$, and it is increasing in $r$ because the function has increasing differences in $(x,z)$ and $z$ is increasing. Replacing $a$ by its right-continuous version, which differs from it on a countable set only, we obtain an element of $\mathcal Q_\infty$ attaining the supremum.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: a first formula for $J$.}\par
Let $z:=p_\rho$ be the quantile function of $\rho$. Inserting \eqref{e.scalar.measure.bidual} into the definition of $J$, where by Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.bidual} the infimum may be restricted to potentials with eventual slope $1$, and exchanging the two suprema,
\begin{equation*}
 J(\rho)=\sup_{a\in\mathcal Q_\infty}\sup_\chi
 \left\{\int_0^1[z(r)a(r)-\chi(a(r))]\,\mathrm d r+g_*(\chi)\right\}
 =\sup_\chi\left\{\int_0^1\chi^*(z(r))\,\mathrm d r+g_*(\chi)\right\},
\end{equation*}
by \eqref{e.pointwise.conjugacy}. Writing $v:=\chi^*|_{[0,1]}\in\mathcal V$, we have $\chi=v^*$ and $\int_0^1v(z(r))\,\mathrm d r=\int v\,\mathrm d\rho$, so that
\begin{equation}\label{e.J.first}
 J(\rho)=\sup_{v\in\mathcal V}\left\{\int v\,\mathrm d\rho+g_*(v^*)\right\}.
\end{equation}

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: proof of \eqref{e.J.star.g} and \eqref{e.J.bidual}.}\par
Since $v^*$ has slope $1$ beyond the Lipschitz constant of $v$, the truncation argument of Lemma~\ref{l.localization} shows that the infimum defining $g_*(v^*)$ may be taken over compactly supported laws, that is, $-g_*(v^*)=\sup_{a\in\mathcal Q_\infty}\{g(a)-\int_0^1v^*(a(r))\,\mathrm d r\}$. Inserting the biconjugacy \eqref{e.scalar.path.bidual} of Lemma~\ref{l.path.bidual} and exchanging the suprema,
\begin{multline*}
 -g_*(v^*)=\sup_{\substack{z\in\mathcal Q_\infty\\0\leqslant z\leqslant1}}
 \left\{\sup_{a\in\mathcal Q_\infty}\int_0^1[z(r)a(r)-v^*(a(r))]\,\mathrm d r-J(z)\right\}\\
 =\sup_z\left\{\int_0^1v(z(r))\,\mathrm d r-J(z)\right\},
\end{multline*}
by \eqref{e.pointwise.conjugacy} applied to $\chi=v^*$, whose conjugate is $v$. Since the paths $z$ in the supremum are exactly the quantile functions of the laws $\rho\in\mathcal P([0,1])$, the right-hand side is $J^*(v)$, which proves \eqref{e.J.star.g}. Inserting \eqref{e.J.star.g} into \eqref{e.J.first} gives \eqref{e.J.bidual}. Finally, $J^*$ is finite by \eqref{e.J.star.g}, since $g(\nu)\leqslant g(\delta_0)+\int x\,\mathrm d\nu$ by the Lipschitz bound of Lemma~\ref{l.legendre.m} and $x-v(1)\leqslant v^*(x)\leqslant x$, so that $-v(1)-g(\delta_0)\leqslant g_*(v^*)\leqslant-g(\delta_0)$; and \eqref{e.def.J.star} represents $J^*$ as a supremum of affine functions of $v$, each $1$-Lipschitz for the uniform norm.

\label{p.hopf.support}
For every $m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, $t\geqslant0$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.hopf.support}
 f_m(t,q)=\mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q).
\end{equation}

\noindent\emph{Step 1: from increasing paths to couplings.}\par
For each $s\in\mathscr S$, let $J_s$ and $J_s^*$ be the functions of Section~\ref{s.duality.scalar} with magnetization $m_s$, and for $z\in[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$, let $\lambda z:=(\lambda_sz_s)_s$. By Lemma~\ref{l.hopf.conjugate}, writing $p=\lambda z$ with $z$ an increasing path with values in $[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$,
\begin{equation*}
 f_m(t,q)=\sup_z\left\{\int_0^1\bigl[q(r)\cdot\lambda z(r)
                            +t\xi(\lambda z(r))\bigr]\,\mathrm d r
                      -\sum_s\lambda_sJ_s(z_s)\right\}.
\end{equation*}
Let $\mu_q$ be the law of $q(U)$ on $\mathbb R_+^{\mathscr S}$, and let $\Pi_q$ be the set of probability measures $\pi$ on $\mathbb R_+^{\mathscr S}\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$ whose first marginal is $\mu_q$. Since $q$ is bounded, every $\pi\in\Pi_q$ is supported in the compact set $K\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$, where $K:=\prod_s[0,\|q_s\|_\infty]$; hence $\Pi_q$ is a convex, weakly closed subset of $\mathcal P(K\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S})$, and thus weakly compact, and the integrands below are bounded and continuous on $K\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$. We write $(x,z)$ for a generic point of $\mathbb R_+^{\mathscr S}\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$, with $x=(x_s)_{s\in\mathscr S}$ and $z=(z_s)_{s\in\mathscr S}$, and for $\pi\in\Pi_q$ and $s\in\mathscr S$, we write $\pi_s\in\mathcal P([0,1])$ for the image of $\pi$ under the map $(x,z)\mapsto z_s$. For an increasing path $z$ as in the last display, let $\pi^z\in\Pi_q$ be the law of $(q(U),z(U))$; we call these couplings monotone. The marginal $\pi^z_s$ is the law of $z_s(U)$, so that $J_s(\pi^z_s)=J_s(z_s)$, and the integral above is $\int[x\cdot\lambda z+t\xi(\lambda z)]\,\mathrm d\pi^z$. We claim that
\begin{equation}\label{e.coupling.formula}
 f_m(t,q)=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_q}
 \left\{\int[x\cdot\lambda z+t\xi(\lambda z)]\,\mathrm d\pi
                             -\sum_s\lambda_sJ_s(\pi_s)\right\},
\end{equation}
that is, that the supremum over all couplings is already attained on the monotone ones. Let $\pi\in\Pi_q$, with marginals $\mu_q$ and $(\rho_s)_s$ for the coordinates $z_s$, and let $\pi^\uparrow:=\pi^z$ for $z:=(p_{\rho_s})_s$, where $p_{\rho_s}$ is the quantile function of $\rho_s$. This monotone coupling has the same marginals as $\pi$, so the costs $J_s$ are unchanged, and it suffices to show that the first integral in \eqref{e.coupling.formula} does not decrease when $\pi$ is replaced by $\pi^\uparrow$. For nonnegative random variables $Y_1,\ldots,Y_n$ with prescribed marginal laws, the layer-cake formula gives
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E\Bigl[\prod_iY_i\Bigr]
 =\int_{\mathbb R_+^n}\mathbb P(Y_1>u_1,\ldots,Y_n>u_n)\,\mathrm d u
 \leqslant\int_{\mathbb R_+^n}\min_i\mathbb P(Y_i>u_i)\,\mathrm d u,
\end{equation*}
and the monotone coupling $Y_i=p_{\operatorname{Law}(Y_i)}(U)$ achieves the bound, since for it the joint tail probability equals the smallest marginal tail probability. We apply this to each product $x_sz_s$ and to each monomial $\prod_sz_s^{a_s}$ in the power series of $\xi(\lambda z)$. Since the coefficients of this series are nonnegative, and since it converges absolutely, summing the inequalities proves the claim.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: exchange of the supremum and the infimum.}\par
By \eqref{e.J.bidual}, $J_s(\pi_s)=\sup_{v_s\in\mathcal V}\{\int v_s\,\mathrm d\pi_s-J_s^*(v_s)\}$, so that \eqref{e.coupling.formula} becomes
\begin{equation}\label{e.minimax}
 f_m(t,q)=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_q}\ \inf_{v\in\mathcal V^{\mathscr S}}
 \Bigl\{\int[x\cdot\lambda z+t\xi(\lambda z)]\,\mathrm d\pi
          -\sum_s\lambda_s\int v_s\,\mathrm d\pi_s
                          +\sum_s\lambda_sJ_s^*(v_s)\Bigr\}.
\end{equation}
The expression in braces is affine in $\pi$, and weakly continuous since its integrands are bounded and continuous on $K\times[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$; it is convex and continuous for the uniform norm in $v$, by Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.conjugacies}. Since $\Pi_q$ is convex and compact, Sion's minimax theorem \cite{sion1958general} allows us to exchange the supremum and the infimum.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: the supremum over couplings at a fixed potential.}\par
Let $v=(v_s)_{s\in\mathscr S}\in\mathcal V^{\mathscr S}$, and let $\chi_v:=\sum_s\lambda_sv_s^*\in\mathfrak X$. Then, for $z\in[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.chi.v.conjugate}
 \chi_v^*(\lambda z)=\sum_s\lambda_sv_s(z_s),
 \qquad
 -(\psi_m)_*(\chi_v)=\sum_s\lambda_sJ_s^*(v_s).
\end{equation}
The first identity follows from $v_s^{**}=v_s$, and the second from \eqref{e.g.conjugate.species} and \eqref{e.J.star.g}. The supremum over $\pi\in\Pi_q$ of the integral of a continuous function of $(x,z)$ is the integral against $\mu_q$ of its pointwise supremum over $z\in[0,1]^{\mathscr S}$. By the first identity in \eqref{e.chi.v.conjugate}, and since $\chi_v^*=+\infty$ outside $\Lambda_\lambda$, the definition \eqref{e.def.potential.evolution} gives
\begin{equation*}
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_q}\int\Bigl[x\cdot\lambda z+t\xi(\lambda z)
                  -\sum_s\lambda_sv_s(z_s)\Bigr]\,\mathrm d\pi(x,z)
 =\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi_v(q(r))\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation*}
Together with the second identity in \eqref{e.chi.v.conjugate}, the exchanged form of \eqref{e.minimax} therefore reads
\begin{equation*}
 f_m(t,q)=\inf_{v\in\mathcal V^{\mathscr S}}
 \left\{\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi_v(q(r))\,\mathrm d r-(\psi_m)_*(\chi_v)\right\}.
\end{equation*}
By Lemma~\ref{l.localization}, the infimum defining $\mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)$ may be restricted to the potentials with slope $1$ beyond the horizons, and these are of the form $\chi_v$ with $v\in\mathcal V^{\mathscr S}$. The infimum over all $v\in\mathcal V^{\mathscr S}$ lies between this restricted infimum and the infimum over all of $\mathfrak X$, so it equals $\mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)$. This proves \eqref{e.hopf.support}.

\label{p.transfer}
For every $m$, $t$, $q$ and $T$ as in \eqref{e.horizon},
\begin{equation}\label{e.transfer}
 \mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)=\inf_{\alpha\in\mathcal M_T^m}
       \left\{\mathcal C_T(\alpha)+\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi_\alpha(q(r))\,\mathrm d r\right\},
\end{equation}
and the infimum is attained.

The compactness used for the attainment is that of the potentials, not of the martingales: we first find an optimal potential, and we then construct a martingale from a law attaining the concave conjugate at this potential.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 1: an optimal potential.}\par
By Lemma~\ref{l.localization}(2), the infimum defining $\mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)$ may be restricted to the potentials $\chi=\sum_s\lambda_s\chi_s$ with $\chi_s\in\mathfrak X_1$ of slope $1$ beyond $T_s$. Such a potential is determined by its restriction to $[0,T]$, and these restrictions form a compact set for the uniform norm, by the Arzel\`a--Ascoli theorem, since they are $1$-Lipschitz, vanish at $0$, and form a closed class. Both terms of the objective in \eqref{e.def.support.value} are $1$-Lipschitz for the uniform norm on $[0,T]$, by \eqref{e.localized.evolution} and \eqref{e.localized.measure.conjugate}. An optimal potential $\chi$ therefore exists; we fix it for the rest of the proof.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 2: from the optimal potential to a martingale.}\par
By \eqref{e.g.conjugate.species} and Lemma~\ref{l.localization}(2), $(\psi_m)_*(\chi)=\sum_s\lambda_s(g_{m_s})_*(\chi_s)$, and each $(g_{m_s})_*(\chi_s)$ is an infimum over $\mathcal P([0,T_s])$, attained at some $\nu_s$. Let $\alpha_s:=M^{\nu_s,m_s}$ be the minimizing martingale of Proposition~\ref{p.scalar.martingale} on $[0,T_s]$, so that $\alpha\in\mathcal M_T^m$ and $g_{m_s}(\nu_s)=c_{T_s}(\alpha_s)+\int\chi_{\alpha_s}\,\mathrm d\nu_s$. We claim that
\begin{equation}\label{e.support.optimality}
 \chi_s(x)-\chi_{\alpha_s}(x)\geqslant\vartheta_s
 \qquad\text{for every }x\in[0,T_s],\qquad
 \vartheta_s:=\int(\chi_s-\chi_{\alpha_s})\,\mathrm d\nu_s.
\end{equation}
This is the first-order optimality condition for $\nu_s$. Indeed, let $x\in[0,T_s]$, $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, and $\nu':=(1-\varepsilon)\nu_s+\varepsilon\delta_x\in\mathcal P([0,T_s])$. By the minimality of $\nu_s$,
\begin{equation*}
 \frac{g_{m_s}(\nu')-g_{m_s}(\nu_s)}{\varepsilon}
 \leqslant\frac1\varepsilon\int\chi_s\,\mathrm d(\nu'-\nu_s)
 =\chi_s(x)-\int\chi_s\,\mathrm d\nu_s,
\end{equation*}
and letting $\varepsilon\to0$ with \eqref{e.scalar.derivative.law} turns the left-hand side into $\chi_{\alpha_s}(x)-\int\chi_{\alpha_s}\,\mathrm d\nu_s$. In words, $\chi_s$ touches $\chi_{\alpha_s}+\vartheta_s$ from above on $[0,T_s]$, and the two integrate to the same value against~$\nu_s$, so $\nu_s$ is supported on the contact set. Consequently,
\begin{equation*}
 (\psi_m)_*(\chi)=\sum_s\lambda_s\Bigl[\int\chi_s\,\mathrm d\nu_s-g_{m_s}(\nu_s)\Bigr]
 =\sum_s\lambda_s\vartheta_s-\mathcal C_T(\alpha),
\end{equation*}
and, since $\chi\geqslant\chi_\alpha+\sum_s\lambda_s\vartheta_s$ on $[0,T]$, the ordering property of Lemma~\ref{l.localization}(1) gives $\mathsf S_t\chi(q(r))\geqslant \mathsf S_t\chi_\alpha(q(r))+\sum_s\lambda_s\vartheta_s$ for every $r$. Therefore
\begin{equation}\label{e.transfer.lower}
 \mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)=\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi(q(r))\,\mathrm d r-(\psi_m)_*(\chi)
 \geqslant\mathcal C_T(\alpha)+\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi_\alpha(q(r))\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation}

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{Step 3: every martingale gives a potential.}\par
Let $\alpha'\in\mathcal M_T^m$ be arbitrary, and let $\widehat\chi_{\alpha'}:=\sum_s\lambda_s\widehat\chi_{\alpha'_s}$, where $\widehat\chi_{\alpha'_s}(x):=\chi_{\alpha'_s}(x\wedge T_s)+(x-T_s)_+$ coincides with $\chi_{\alpha'_s}$ on $[0,T_s]$ and has slope $1$ beyond $T_s$. Let $\nu\in\mathcal P_1(\mathbb R_+^{\mathscr S})$, and let $\nu^T$ be its image under the coordinatewise truncation $x\mapsto x\wedge T$. By Lemma~\ref{l.legendre.m}, $g_{m_s}(\nu_s)\leqslant g_{m_s}(\nu^T_s)+\int(x_s-T_s)_+\,\mathrm d\nu_s$, and by the inequality in \eqref{e.scalar.martingale} at $\nu^T_s\in\mathcal P([0,T_s])$, $g_{m_s}(\nu^T_s)\leqslant c_{T_s}(\alpha'_s)+\int\chi_{\alpha'_s}\,\mathrm d\nu^T_s$. Since $\int\chi_{\alpha'_s}\,\mathrm d\nu^T_s+\int(x_s-T_s)_+\,\mathrm d\nu_s=\int\widehat\chi_{\alpha'_s}\,\mathrm d\nu_s$, this gives $g_{m_s}(\nu_s)\leqslant c_{T_s}(\alpha'_s)+\int\widehat\chi_{\alpha'_s}\,\mathrm d\nu_s$, hence $\psi_m(\nu)\leqslant\mathcal C_T(\alpha')+\int\widehat\chi_{\alpha'}\,\mathrm d\nu$ and $(\psi_m)_*(\widehat\chi_{\alpha'})\geqslant-\mathcal C_T(\alpha')$. Moreover, $\mathsf S_t\widehat\chi_{\alpha'}(q(r))=\mathsf S_t\chi_{\alpha'}(q(r))$ by Lemma~\ref{l.localization}(1). Thus
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)\leqslant\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\widehat\chi_{\alpha'}(q(r))\,\mathrm d r-(\psi_m)_*(\widehat\chi_{\alpha'})
 \leqslant\mathcal C_T(\alpha')+\int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi_{\alpha'}(q(r))\,\mathrm d r.
\end{equation*}
Together with \eqref{e.transfer.lower}, this proves \eqref{e.transfer}, with the infimum attained at the martingale $\alpha$ of Step~2.

Let $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$ and let $T$ satisfy \eqref{e.horizon}. We claim that $\mathsf S_t\chi_\alpha(q(r))=\mathcal K^{T}_{t,\alpha}(q(r))$ for every $\alpha\in\mathcal M_T$ and $r\in[0,1]$. Since $q_s(r)\leqslant\|q_s\|_\infty$, the point $x=q(r)$ satisfies the condition of Lemma~\ref{l.localization}(1), so $\mathsf S_t\chi_\alpha(q(r))$ is given by \eqref{e.localized.evolution}, which only involves the conjugate of $\chi_\alpha$ truncated to $[0,T]$. This truncated conjugate is explicit: for an increasing function $\varrho:[0,T_s]\to[0,1]$ and $z\in[0,1]$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.explicit.conjugate}
 \sup_{0\leqslant y\leqslant T_s}\left\{zy-\int_0^y\varrho(v)\,\mathrm d v\right\}
                             =\int_0^{T_s}(z-\varrho(v))_+\,\mathrm d v,
\end{equation}
since the function of $y$ on the left increases while $\varrho<z$ and decreases afterwards. Applying this in each coordinate of \eqref{e.localized.evolution}, with $p=\lambda z$ and $\varrho(v)=\mathbb E[\alpha_s(v)^2]$, which is increasing since $\alpha_s$ is a martingale, and comparing with \eqref{e.def.K}, proves the claim. Combining it with Propositions~\ref{p.hopf.support} and~\ref{p.transfer} gives
\begin{equation*}
 f_m(t,q)=\inf_{\alpha\in\mathcal M_T^m}
 \left\{\mathcal C_T(\alpha)+\int_0^1\mathcal K^{T}_{t,\alpha}(q(r))\,\mathrm d r\right\},
\end{equation*}
with the infimum attained. This completes the proof of Theorem~\ref{t.slices}.

\label{t.enriched}
Let $t\geqslant0$ and $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$. For every $q\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$, the identity \eqref{e.full.slices} holds, and the infimum there is attained. For every $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$ and every $T$ satisfying \eqref{e.horizon}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{e.main.enriched}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\overline F_N(t,q)
 =\inf_{\alpha\in\mathcal M_T}
 \left\{\mathcal C_T(\alpha)-\sum_s\lambda_sh_s\alpha_s(0)
                       +\int_0^1\mathcal K^{T}_{t,\alpha}(q(r))\,\mathrm d r\right\},
\end{equation}
and the infimum is attained.

We start with the proof of \eqref{e.full.slices}. 
Let $t\geqslant0$, $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_2^{\mathscr S}$. By Lemma~\ref{l.gluing},
\begin{equation*}
 \overline F_N(t,q)
 -\min_k\bigl\{\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-\lambda_N\cdot(hm_{N,k})\bigr\}
 \longrightarrow0.
\end{equation*}
By Theorem~\ref{t.slices}, $\sup_k|\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-f_{m_{N,k}}(t,q)|\to0$, the function $m\mapsto f_m(t,q)$ is continuous on $[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, the set of compositions $\{m_{N,k}\}_k$ becomes dense in $[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$ as $N\to\infty$, and $\lambda_N\to\lambda$. Hence
\begin{equation*}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\min_k\bigl\{\overline F_{N,k}(t,q)-\lambda_N\cdot(hm_{N,k})\bigr\}
 =\inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}\bigl\{f_m(t,q)-\lambda\cdot(hm)\bigr\},
\end{equation*}
where the infimum on the right is attained by continuity and compactness. This shows~\eqref{e.full.slices}.

Turning now to the proof of \eqref{e.main.enriched}, we let $t\geqslant0$, $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$, and let $T$ satisfy~\eqref{e.horizon}. By \eqref{e.slice.martingale}, we have for every $m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$ that
\begin{equation*}
 f_m(t,q)-\lambda\cdot(hm)
 =\inf_{\alpha\in\mathcal M_T^m}\left\{
 \mathcal C_T(\alpha)-\sum_s\lambda_sh_s\alpha_s(0)
                       +\int_0^1\mathcal K^{T}_{t,\alpha}(q(r))\,\mathrm d r\right\},
\end{equation*}
since $\alpha_s(0)=m_s$ on $\mathcal M_T^m$. The sets $\mathcal M_T^m$, $m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, form a partition of $\mathcal M_T$, because the initial value of a martingale in $\mathcal M_T$ is deterministic. Minimizing over $m$ in \eqref{e.full.slices} therefore gives \eqref{e.main.enriched}, and the infimum is attained: choose a minimizing $m$ in \eqref{e.full.slices}, and then a minimizing martingale in $\mathcal M_T^m$.

\label{t.potential}
Let $t\geqslant0$ and $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$. For every $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$, 
\begin{equation}\label{e.potential.formula}
 \lim_{N\to\infty}\overline F_N(t,q)=\mathcal U_t[\psi^h](q)
 =\inf_{\chi\in\mathfrak X}\left\{
             \int_0^1\mathsf S_t\chi(q(r))\,\mathrm d r-(\psi^h)_*(\chi)\right\}.
\end{equation}

Let $t\geqslant0$, $h\in\mathbb R^{\mathscr S}$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_\infty^{\mathscr S}$. By Lemma~\ref{l.legendre.m} applied in each coordinate,
\begin{equation*}
 \psi^h(\nu)=\inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}
                       \{\psi_m(\nu)-\lambda\cdot(hm)\}
\end{equation*}
for every law $\nu$. Each $\psi_\circ(\cdot;h_s)$ is $1$-Lipschitz for $W_1$ by Lemma~\ref{l.scalar.continuity}, so Lemma~\ref{l.localization} applies to $\psi^h$: the infimum in \eqref{e.potential.formula} may be restricted to potentials $\chi$ with slope $1$ beyond the horizons $T$ of \eqref{e.horizon}, and for these potentials,
\begin{equation*}
 -(\psi^h)_*(\chi)
 =\sup_{\nu\in\mathcal P([0,T])}\ \inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}
 \Bigl\{\psi_m(\nu)-\lambda\cdot(hm)-\int\chi\,\mathrm d\nu\Bigr\}.
\end{equation*}
The function in braces is continuous on the compact convex set $\mathcal P([0,T])\times[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}$, by the Lipschitz bounds in $\nu$ and in $m$ of Lemma~\ref{l.legendre.m}; it is concave in $\nu$ by Corollary~\ref{c.scalar.support}, and convex in $m$ by Lemma~\ref{l.legendre.m}. By Sion's minimax theorem, the supremum and the infimum may be exchanged, and Lemma~\ref{l.localization} applied to $\psi_m$ then gives
\begin{equation*}
 -(\psi^h)_*(\chi)=\inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}
                       \{-(\psi_m)_*(\chi)-\lambda\cdot(hm)\}.
\end{equation*}
Taking the infimum over $\chi$ and exchanging the two infima, we obtain, by Proposition~\ref{p.hopf.support},
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal U_t[\psi^h](q)=\inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}
                    \{\mathcal U_t[\psi_m](q)-\lambda\cdot(hm)\}
 =\inf_{m\in[-1,1]^{\mathscr S}}\{f_m(t,q)-\lambda\cdot(hm)\},
\end{equation*}
and \eqref{e.potential.formula} follows from \eqref{e.full.slices}.

\label{l.recursion}
For every $x\in\mathbb R^N$ and $0\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.recursion}
 U^{(n)}(r_j,x;g)=T_{r_j,\,q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j)}U^{(n)}(r_{j+1},\cdot\,;g)(x),
\end{equation}
with $U^{(n)}(1,\cdot\,;g)$ the terminal function \eqref{e.def.terminal}. Moreover, let $X^{(n)}$ solve \eqref{e.branch.sde} with $\rho_n(r)$ in place of the coefficient $r$ and $U^{(n)}$ in place of $U$, driven by $X_0$ and $B$. For every realization of $g$, the law of $X^{(n)}$ has the density
\begin{equation}\label{e.frozen.density}
\begin{split}
 \Lambda_n(x;g)
 &:=\prod_{j=1}^{n-1}\exp\Bigl(r_j\bigl[U^{(n)}(r_{j+1},x_{r_{j+1}};g)-U^{(n)}(r_j,x_{r_j};g)\bigr]\Bigr)\\
 &\;=\exp\Bigl(\Bigl(1-\frac1n\Bigr)U(1,x_1;g)-\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}U^{(n)}(r_j,x_{r_j};g)\Bigr)
\end{split}
\end{equation}
at the path $x$ with respect to $\gamma_q$. In particular, the values $(X^{(n)}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_n})$ form a Markov chain, whose initial value $X^{(n)}_{r_1}$ is a centered Gaussian vector with covariance $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_1))$, and in which, for $1\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$ and given $X^{(n)}_{r_j}=x$, the increment $X^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}}-x$ has density
\begin{equation}\label{e.selected.transition}
 \exp\Bigl(r_j\bigl[U^{(n)}(r_{j+1},x+z;g)-U^{(n)}(r_j,x;g)\bigr]\Bigr)
\end{equation}
at $z$ with respect to the centered Gaussian law with covariance $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j))$. Conditionally on these values, $X^{(n)}$ has the conditional law of the Gaussian path $Z$ given the same values.

On $[r_j,r_{j+1}]$ the coefficient is the constant $r_j$, and for $j\geqslant1$ the function $\exp(r_jU^{(n)})$ solves the backward heat equation $\partial_rw+\operatorname{tr}(D\nabla^2w)=0$, whose solution is the Gaussian convolution with covariance $2\int_{r_j}^{r_{j+1}}D=2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j))$. For $j=0$ the equation for $U^{(n)}$ itself is this heat equation. This proves \eqref{e.recursion}.

For the density, we repeat the Girsanov computation of the proof of Lemma~\ref{l.joint.density} with the coefficient $\rho_n$. Given $g$, the frozen branch has the bounded drift $2\rho_nD\nabla U^{(n)}$ relative to the Gaussian path, so its law has density
\begin{equation*}
 \exp\left(\int_0^1\rho_n(r)\,\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot\sqrt{2D}\,\mathrm d\widetilde B_r
          -\int_0^1\rho_n(r)^2\,\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot D\nabla U^{(n)}\,\mathrm d r\right)
\end{equation*}
with respect to $\gamma_q$, where $\mathrm d x_r=\sqrt{2D(r)}\,\mathrm d\widetilde B_r$ under $\gamma_q$, and where $U^{(n)}$ is evaluated at $(r,x_r;g)$. Under $\gamma_q$, It\^o's formula and the frozen equation give $\mathrm d U^{(n)}(r,x_r;g)=-\rho_n(r)\,\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot D\nabla U^{(n)}\,\mathrm d r+\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot\sqrt{2D}\,\mathrm d\widetilde B_r$ on each interval between consecutive mesh points and breakpoints of $q$, and $U^{(n)}$ is continuous across these points. Since $\rho_n=r_j$ on $[r_j,r_{j+1})$ and $r_0=0$, the exponent equals
\begin{equation*}
 \sum_{j=1}^{n-1}r_j\bigl[U^{(n)}(r_{j+1},x_{r_{j+1}};g)-U^{(n)}(r_j,x_{r_j};g)\bigr],
\end{equation*}
which is the first expression in \eqref{e.frozen.density}; the second follows by summation by parts, using $r_j-r_{j-1}=1/n$ and $U^{(n)}(1,\cdot\,;g)=U(1,\cdot\,;g)$. Under $\gamma_q$, the value $x_{r_1}=X_0+(Z_{r_1}-Z_0)$ is a centered Gaussian vector with covariance $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_1))$, and the increments $x_{r_{j+1}}-x_{r_j}$, $1\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$, are independent of it and of each other, centered Gaussian with covariances $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j))$. The density $\Lambda_n$ depends on the path only through its values at the mesh points, and it is a product of functions of two consecutive values, each of which integrates to one in the later value by \eqref{e.recursion}. The two remaining assertions follow.

\label{l.frozen.convergence}
Let $\|\dot q\|_\infty$ denote the largest slope of $q$ over all pieces and species. For every $n\geqslant1$ and every realization of $g$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.frozen.convergence}
 \sup_{[0,1]\times\mathbb R^N}\bigl|U^{(n)}-U\bigr|\leqslant\frac{N\|\dot q\|_\infty}{n}.
\end{equation}

Since $U^{(n)}$ solves the frozen equation, the operator \eqref{e.def.branch.operator} gives
\begin{equation*}
 \mathcal L[U^{(n)}]=\bigl(r-\rho_n(r)\bigr)\,\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot D(r)\nabla U^{(n)}
 \in\Bigl[0,\frac{N\|\dot q\|_\infty}{n}\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
because $0\leqslant r-\rho_n(r)\leqslant1/n$ and $|\nabla U^{(n)}|^2\leqslant N$, while $\mathcal L[U]=0$ and the two functions have the same terminal datum. The estimate \eqref{e.comparison} of Lemma~\ref{l.comparison}, applied on the pieces delimited by the breakpoints of $q$ and the mesh points, gives \eqref{e.frozen.convergence}.

\label{l.selected.leaf}
Let $q$ be regular, let $n\geqslant1$, $r_j:=j/n$, and let $\overline q^{(n)}$ be the step path with the values $q_j:=q(r_j)$ on $[r_{j-1},r_j)$, $1\leqslant j\leqslant n$. Consider the cascade associated with $\overline q^{(n)}$, a leaf $\alpha$ selected according to the Gibbs weights, and a configuration $\sigma$ drawn from \eqref{e.leaf.configuration}. For every realization of $g$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\log\sum_\alpha\mathsf v_\alpha e^{U(1,x_\alpha;g)}=\mathbb E\bigl[U^{(n)}(0,X_0;g)\bigm|g\bigr]$, where $X_0\sim\mathcal N(0,2\operatorname{diag}(q(0)))$ is the initial value of the branch, independent of $g$;
\item under $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}$, the fields $(x_{\alpha_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha_{|n-1}})$ along the selected leaf and the configuration~$\sigma$ have the law of $(X^{(n)}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_n},\sigma^{(n)})$, where $X^{(n)}$ is the frozen branch of Lemma~\ref{l.recursion}, and where $\sigma^{(n)}$ is drawn, given $X^{(n)}$, from the Gibbs measure \eqref{e.sampled.configuration} in the terminal field $X^{(n)}_1$.
\end{enumerate}

We use the invariance of Poisson--Dirichlet cascades under a tilt of the weights by a function of the marks, \cite[Lemma~3]{pantal07}, through its two consequences \cite[(3.13) and Theorem~6]{pantal07}; the first one is the recursive formula of \cite[Theorem~5.25]{HJbook}, and the formulation with a mark at the root that we use is that of \cite[Appendix~A]{chen2026spherical}. Fix $g$ and the root mark $z_\emptyset$, and regard $\Theta_\alpha:=U(1,x_\alpha;g)$ as a function of the marks $(z_{\alpha_{|1}},\ldots,z_{\alpha_{|n-1}})$ along the leaf $\alpha$. This function is Lipschitz, since $|\nabla U(1,\cdot\,;g)|\leqslant\sqrt N$, so that $e^{\Theta_\alpha}$ is integrable, as required in \cite{pantal07}. With the parameters $m_j=r_j$, the recursion (3.4) of \cite{pantal07} sets $\Theta_{\alpha_{|n-1}}:=\Theta_\alpha$ and, for $j$ from $n-1$ down to $1$,
\begin{equation*}
 \Theta_{\alpha_{|j-1}}:=\frac1{r_j}\log\mathbb E_j\exp\bigl(r_j\Theta_{\alpha_{|j}}\bigr),
\end{equation*}
where $\mathbb E_j$ integrates the mark $z_{\alpha_{|j}}$ only. Since $x_{\alpha_{|j}}=x_{\alpha_{|j-1}}+\sqrt{2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j))}\,z_{\alpha_{|j}}$ by \eqref{e.cascade.fields}, a backward induction based on \eqref{e.recursion} gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.recursion.dictionary}
 \Theta_{\alpha_{|j}}=U^{(n)}(r_{j+1},x_{\alpha_{|j}};g),\qquad0\leqslant j\leqslant n-1,
\end{equation}
and the weights $\mathsf v_\alpha e^{\Theta_\alpha}/\sum_\beta\mathsf v_\beta e^{\Theta_\beta}$ of the tilted cascade in \cite{pantal07} are the Gibbs weights \eqref{e.cascade.gibbs}. In particular, $\Theta_{\alpha_{|0}}=U^{(n)}(r_1,x_\emptyset;g)$ depends on the root mark only, and \cite[(3.13)]{pantal07} gives
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\Bigl[\log\sum_\alpha\mathsf v_\alpha e^{U(1,x_\alpha;g)}\Bigm|z_\emptyset\Bigr]
 =U^{(n)}(r_1,x_\emptyset;g).
\end{equation*}
Since $x_\emptyset=\sqrt{2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_1))}\,z_\emptyset$ has the law of $X_0+\sqrt{2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_1)-q(0))}\,z$, where $z$ is a standard Gaussian vector independent of $X_0$ and of $g$, averaging over $z_\emptyset$ and using the case $j=0$ of \eqref{e.recursion}, in which $T_{r_0,a}=T_{0,a}$ is a plain Gaussian expectation, gives (1). Next, \cite[Theorem~6]{pantal07} states that, conditionally on $z_\emptyset$ and under $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}$, the marks $(z_{\alpha_{|1}},\ldots,z_{\alpha_{|n-1}})$ along the selected leaf have the law under which, for $1\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$ and given the marks of smaller depth, $z_{\alpha_{|j}}$ has density $\exp(r_j[\Theta_{\alpha_{|j}}-\Theta_{\alpha_{|j-1}}])$ with respect to the standard Gaussian law. By \eqref{e.recursion.dictionary} and \eqref{e.cascade.fields}, this means that, given $x_{\alpha_{|j-1}}=x$, the increment $x_{\alpha_{|j}}-x$ has density \eqref{e.selected.transition} with respect to the centered Gaussian law with covariance $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j))$, while $x_{\alpha_{|0}}=x_\emptyset$ is a centered Gaussian vector with covariance $2\operatorname{diag}(q(r_1))$. Thus the fields along the selected leaf form the Markov chain of Lemma~\ref{l.recursion}, and $(x_{\alpha_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha_{|n-1}})$ has the law of $(X^{(n)}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_n})$. Finally, the conditional law \eqref{e.leaf.configuration} of the configuration depends on the environment and on the leaf only through $x_\alpha=x_{\alpha_{|n-1}}$, and it is the conditional law of $\sigma^{(n)}$ given $X^{(n)}_1=x_\alpha$. This proves (2).

\label{l.selected.pair}
Consider the setting of Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}, and let $0\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$. For every realization of $g$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}(J=j)=1/n$;
\item conditionally on $J=j$ and under $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}$, the fields along the two selected leaves and the two configurations, that is, the two families $(x_{\alpha^a_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha^a_{|n-1}},\sigma^a)$, $a=1,2$, have the law of the two families $(X^{(n),a}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n),a}_{r_n},\sigma^{(n),a})$, $a=1,2$, where the two frozen branches coincide up to the level $r_{j+1}$ and are driven by independent Brownian motions afterwards, and where the configurations are drawn independently in their terminal fields, as above;
\item consequently, for every $b:\Omega^2\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.selected.pair}
 \mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\1_{\{J=j\}}\bigr]
 =\frac1n\,\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}}\bigm|g\bigr].
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

We keep the notation of the proof of Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}: $g$ and the root mark $z_\emptyset$ are fixed, the recursion (3.4) of \cite{pantal07} with the parameters $m_\ell=r_\ell$ produces $\Theta_{\alpha_{|\ell}}=U^{(n)}(r_{\ell+1},x_{\alpha_{|\ell}};g)$, the weights of the tilted cascade are the Gibbs weights \eqref{e.cascade.gibbs}, and the factor $W_{\alpha_{|\ell}}:=\exp(r_\ell[\Theta_{\alpha_{|\ell}}-\Theta_{\alpha_{|\ell-1}}])$ of \cite[(3.5)]{pantal07} is, by \eqref{e.recursion.dictionary} and \eqref{e.cascade.fields}, the density \eqref{e.selected.transition} of the increment $x_{\alpha_{|\ell}}-x_{\alpha_{|\ell-1}}$ given $x_{\alpha_{|\ell-1}}$, with respect to its Gaussian law. In \cite{pantal07}, the overlap of two distinct leaves is the index of the first coordinate at which they differ, which is $J+1$ in our notation. Let $Y$ be a bounded function of the marks along two leaves. For $0\leqslant j\leqslant n-2$, \cite[Theorem~7]{pantal07} gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.pantal.pair}
 \mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[Y\1_{\{J=j\}}\bigr]
 =(r_{j+1}-r_j)\,
 \mathbb E\Bigl[\prod_{\ell=1}^{j}W_{\alpha_{|\ell}}
         \prod_{\ell=j+1}^{n-1}W_{\alpha^1_{|\ell}}W_{\alpha^2_{|\ell}}\;Y\Bigr],
\end{equation}
where, on the right, $\alpha^1$ and $\alpha^2$ are two fixed leaves whose most recent common ancestor $\alpha_{|j}:=\alpha^1_{|j}=\alpha^2_{|j}$ has depth $j$, and the marks are independent standard Gaussian vectors, the two leaves sharing the marks of depth at most $j$. For $j=n-1$, the invariance \cite[(3.14)]{pantal07} of the pair formed by the weights and the marks, which is the content of \cite[Lemma~3]{pantal07}, gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.pantal.same.leaf}
 \mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[Y\1_{\{\alpha^1=\alpha^2\}}\bigr]
 =\mathbb E\Bigl[\sum_\alpha\mathsf v_\alpha^2\Bigr]\,
  \mathbb E\Bigl[\prod_{\ell=1}^{n-1}W_{\alpha_{|\ell}}\;Y\Bigr],
\end{equation}
with a single fixed leaf $\alpha$ on the right, since the weights and the marks of the second process in \cite[(3.14)]{pantal07} are independent; and $\mathbb E\sum_\alpha\mathsf v_\alpha^2=1-r_{n-1}=1/n$, by \cite[(3.17)]{pantal07} summed over the overlap. Since $r_{j+1}-r_j=1/n$, taking $Y\equiv1$ in \eqref{e.pantal.pair} and \eqref{e.pantal.same.leaf} proves (1).

The density on the right of \eqref{e.pantal.pair}, multiplied by the Gaussian law of the marks and read through \eqref{e.cascade.fields}, is the following law of the fields along the two leaves: the common fields $(x_{\alpha_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha_{|j}})$ have the law of $(X^{(n)}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}})$, since $x_{\alpha_{|0}}=x_\emptyset$ has the law of $X^{(n)}_{r_1}$ once $z_\emptyset$ is averaged, and since the factors $W_{\alpha_{|\ell}}$, $\ell\leqslant j$, are the transition densities \eqref{e.selected.transition} of the chain of Lemma~\ref{l.recursion}; and, given the common fields, the two families $(x_{\alpha^a_{|j+1}},\ldots,x_{\alpha^a_{|n-1}})$, $a=1,2$, are independent, each with the conditional law of $(X^{(n)}_{r_{j+2}},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_n})$ given $X^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}}=x_{\alpha_{|j}}$, by the same transition densities, each factor integrating to one in the next mark by \eqref{e.recursion}. On the side of the branch, $X^{(n),1}$ and $X^{(n),2}$ coincide up to the level $r_{j+1}$, and, given $\mathcal G_{r_{j+1}}$, their values at the later mesh points are two independent copies of the chain started from $X^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}}$, by the Markov property of the frozen equation and the independence of the two driving noises after $r_{j+1}$. The laws of the fields in (2) thus agree. The conditional law \eqref{e.leaf.configuration} of each configuration depends on the environment and on its leaf only through the terminal field of that leaf, and the two configurations are drawn independently; the same holds for $\sigma^{(n),1},\sigma^{(n),2}$. This proves (2), and (3) follows from (1), (2) and the identity $\mathbb E[\langle b\rangle^{(n)}_{u}\mid g]=\mathbb E[b(\sigma^{(n),1},\sigma^{(n),2})\mid g]$ recalled before the statement.

\label{p.selected.limit}
Let $q$ be regular, and for $n\geqslant1$ let $\alpha$ be a leaf selected according to the Gibbs weights and $\sigma$ the configuration drawn at this leaf, as in Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}. For every realization of $g$, the law under $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}$ of the fields along the selected leaf and of its configuration converges in total variation, as $n\to\infty$, to the conditional law given $g$ of the values of the branch $X$ at the mesh points $r_1,\ldots,r_n$ and of its sampled configuration \eqref{e.sampled.configuration}, that is,
\begin{equation}\label{e.selected.limit}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\ \sup_{f}\,
 \Bigl|\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[f(x_{\alpha_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha_{|n-1}},\sigma)\bigr]
       -\mathbb E\bigl[f(X_{r_1},\ldots,X_{r_n},\sigma)\bigm|g\bigr]\Bigr|=0,
\end{equation}
where the supremum is over all measurable $f:(\mathbb R^N)^n\times\Omega\to[-1,1]$. The statement remains true after averaging over $g$, with $f$ then also allowed to depend on $g$.

Let $X^{(n)}$ and $\sigma^{(n)}$ be the frozen branch of Lemma~\ref{l.recursion} and its sampled configuration, as in Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(2), so that the law of $(x_{\alpha_{|0}},\ldots,x_{\alpha_{|n-1}},\sigma)$ under $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}$ is the conditional law of $(X^{(n)}_{r_1},\ldots,X^{(n)}_{r_n},\sigma^{(n)})$ given $g$. The two laws compared in \eqref{e.selected.limit} are thus the images of the conditional laws of $(X^{(n)},\sigma^{(n)})$ and of $(X,\sigma)$ given $g$ under the map $(x,\sigma)\mapsto(x_{r_1},\ldots,x_{r_n},\sigma)$, and it suffices to show that the conditional law of $(X^{(n)},\sigma^{(n)})$ given $g$ converges in total variation to the conditional law of $(X,\sigma)$ given $g$.

The proof of Lemma~\ref{l.joint.density} uses neither the slice nor the value of the weights, and the Girsanov density there is computed at fixed $g$: for every $(\Omega,w)$ and every realization of $g$, the conditional law of $(X,\sigma)$ given $g$ has density
\begin{equation}\label{e.general.joint.density}
 p(g,x,\sigma):=w(\sigma)\exp\left(\sqrt{2t}\,g\cdot v_\sigma+x_1\cdot\sigma
                         -\int_0^1U(r,x_r;g)\,\mathrm d r\right)
\end{equation}
at $(x,\sigma)$ with respect to the product of $\gamma_q$ and the counting measure on $\Omega$. Likewise, multiplying the density \eqref{e.frozen.density} of the frozen branch by the Gibbs probability \eqref{e.sampled.configuration} of $\sigma^{(n)}$ given $X^{(n)}_1$, which contains the factor $e^{-U(1,x_1;g)}$, we see that the conditional law of $(X^{(n)},\sigma^{(n)})$ given $g$ has density
\begin{equation}\label{e.frozen.joint.density}
 p_n(g,x,\sigma):=w(\sigma)\exp\left(\sqrt{2t}\,g\cdot v_\sigma+x_1\cdot\sigma
                         -\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nU^{(n)}(r_j,x_{r_j};g)\right)
\end{equation}
with respect to the same reference measure, the term $j=n$ of the sum being $U(1,x_1;g)$. For every $g$, every continuous path $x$ and every $\sigma$, we have $p_n(g,x,\sigma)\to p(g,x,\sigma)$ as $n\to\infty$: by Lemma~\ref{l.frozen.convergence}, the sum in \eqref{e.frozen.joint.density} differs by at most $N\|\dot q\|_\infty/n$ from $\frac1n\sum_{j=1}^nU(r_j,x_{r_j};g)$, which is a Riemann sum of the function $r\mapsto U(r,x_r;g)$, continuous on $[0,1]$ by Lemma~\ref{l.wellposed}. For every $g$, both $p_n(g,\cdot,\cdot)$ and $p(g,\cdot,\cdot)$ are probability densities with respect to the same measure, so Scheff\'e's lemma gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.tv.fixed.g}
 \varepsilon_n(g):=\int\sum_{\sigma\in\Omega}\bigl|p_n(g,x,\sigma)-p(g,x,\sigma)\bigr|\,\gamma_q(\mathrm d x)
 \longrightarrow0,
\end{equation}
and $\varepsilon_n(g)$ is twice the total variation distance between the conditional laws of $(X^{(n)},\sigma^{(n)})$ and $(X,\sigma)$ given $g$. The supremum in \eqref{e.selected.limit} is therefore at most $\varepsilon_n(g)$, and tends to zero. Since $\gamma$ is the product of the law of $g$ and of $\gamma_q$, the total variation distance between the laws of $(g,X^{(n)},\sigma^{(n)})$ and $(g,X,\sigma)$ is $\frac12\mathbb E\,\varepsilon_n(g)$, which tends to $0$ by dominated convergence, since $\varepsilon_n\leqslant2$; this gives the statement averaged over $g$.

\label{p.cascade.pair}
Let $q$ be regular, let $b:\Omega^2\to\mathbb R$, and for $n\geqslant1$ let $(\alpha^1,\sigma^1)$, $(\alpha^2,\sigma^2)$ and $J$ be as in Lemma~\ref{l.selected.pair}. For every realization of $g$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.cascade.pair.uniform}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\ \max_{0\leqslant j\leqslant n-1}\ \sup_{u\in[r_j,r_{j+1}]}\,
 \Bigl|\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\bigm|J=j\bigr]
       -\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_u\bigm|g\bigr]\Bigr|=0,
\end{equation}
and consequently, for every continuous $\varphi:[0,1]\to\mathbb R$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.cascade.pair.integrated}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[\varphi(r_J)\,b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\bigr]
 =\int_0^1\varphi(r)\,\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_r\bigm|g\bigr]\,\mathrm d r .
\end{equation}
Both statements remain true after averaging over $g$; since $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}(J=j)=1/n$ does not depend on $g$, the averaged conditional expectation in \eqref{e.cascade.pair.uniform} is the conditional expectation given $J=j$ under the law that also averages over $g$.

Fix a realization of $g$, and let $\delta_n(g)$ denote the supremum in \eqref{e.selected.limit}, so that $\delta_n(g)\to0$ by Proposition~\ref{p.selected.limit}. We proceed in three steps.

\emph{Step 1: stability of two replicas.} For a signed measure $\rho$, let $\|\rho\|$ denote the total mass of $|\rho|$, so that $|\int b\,\mathrm d\rho|\leqslant\|b\|_\infty\|\rho\|$. Let $\mu,\mu'$ be probability measures on $\mathbb R^N$, and let $\mathsf k,\mathsf k'$ be probability kernels from $\mathbb R^N$ to $\Omega$. We claim that
\begin{equation}\label{e.kernel.stability}
 \bigl\|\mu(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k(x,\cdot)^{\otimes2}-\mu'(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k'(x,\cdot)^{\otimes2}\bigr\|
 \leqslant5\,\bigl\|\mu(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k(x,\cdot)-\mu'(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k'(x,\cdot)\bigr\| .
\end{equation}
Indeed, for probability measures $\rho,\rho'$ on $\Omega$, we have $\|\rho^{\otimes2}-\rho'^{\otimes2}\|\leqslant\|(\rho-\rho')\otimes\rho\|+\|\rho'\otimes(\rho-\rho')\|=2\|\rho-\rho'\|$. Hence the left side of \eqref{e.kernel.stability} is at most
\begin{equation*}
 \|\mu-\mu'\|+\int\mu'(\mathrm d x)\,\bigl\|\mathsf k(x,\cdot)^{\otimes2}-\mathsf k'(x,\cdot)^{\otimes2}\bigr\|
 \leqslant\|\mu-\mu'\|+2\,\bigl\|\mu'(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k(x,\cdot)-\mu'(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k'(x,\cdot)\bigr\|,
\end{equation*}
and the last norm is at most $\|\mu'-\mu\|$ plus the right side of \eqref{e.kernel.stability} without the factor $5$. Since $\|\mu-\mu'\|$ is the norm of the difference of the first marginals, it is also at most this right side, and \eqref{e.kernel.stability} follows.

\emph{Step 2: uniform convergence of the two-replica averages.} For $u\in[0,1]$, let $\mu_u$ be the conditional law of $X_u$ given $g$. By the Markov property of the branch, the conditional law of $\sigma$ given $\mathcal G_u$ is a function of $X_u$: there is a probability kernel $\mathsf k_u$ from $\mathbb R^N$ to $\Omega$, namely $\mathsf k_u(x,\tau)$ is the probability that the configuration sampled in the terminal field of the branch started from $(u,x)$ equals $\tau$, such that $\mathbb P(\sigma=\tau\mid\mathcal G_u)=\mathsf k_u(X_u,\tau)$ for every $\tau\in\Omega$. By \eqref{e.response.first} applied to $b=\1_{\{\sigma=\tau\}}$ and the proof of Lemma~\ref{l.response}, $\mathsf k_u(x,\tau)$ is the value at $(u,x)$ of the solution of a linear parabolic equation, and $(u,x)\mapsto\mathsf k_u(x,\tau)$ is continuous and bounded on $[0,1]\times\mathbb R^N$. Hence the conditional law of $(X_u,\sigma)$ given $g$ is $\mu_u(\mathrm d x)\,\mathsf k_u(x,\cdot)$, and, by the definition of $\langle\cdot\rangle_u$,
\begin{equation*}
 \mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_u\bigm|g\bigr]
 =\int\mu_u(\mathrm d x)\sum_{\tau,\tau'\in\Omega}b(\tau,\tau')\,\mathsf k_u(x,\tau)\,\mathsf k_u(x,\tau').
\end{equation*}
The same holds for the frozen branch, with the conditional law $\mu^{(n)}_u$ of $X^{(n)}_u$ given $g$ and the kernel $\mathsf k^{(n)}_u$ defined with the frozen equation. At a mesh point $u=r_{j+1}$, $0\leqslant j\leqslant n-1$, the conditional law of $(X^{(n)}_u,\sigma^{(n)})$ given $g$ is the law of $(x_{\alpha_{|j}},\sigma)$ under $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}$, by Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(2), so the conditional laws of $(X^{(n)}_u,\sigma^{(n)})$ and $(X_u,\sigma)$ given $g$ are the images, under the projection onto the $(j+1)$-th field and the configuration, of the two laws compared in \eqref{e.selected.limit}. The norm of their difference is thus at most $\delta_n(g)$, and \eqref{e.kernel.stability} gives
\begin{equation}\label{e.pair.uniform.n}
 \max_{1\leqslant j\leqslant n}\Bigl|\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle^{(n)}_{r_j}\bigm|g\bigr]-\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_{r_j}\bigm|g\bigr]\Bigr|
 \leqslant5\,\|b\|_\infty\,\delta_n(g).
\end{equation}

\emph{Step 3: conclusion.} Since $(u,x)\mapsto\mathsf k_u(x,\tau)$ is continuous and bounded for every $\tau$ and $u\mapsto X_u$ is continuous, the function $u\mapsto\mathbb E[\langle b\rangle_u\mid g]=\mathbb E[\sum_{\tau,\tau'}b(\tau,\tau')\mathsf k_u(X_u,\tau)\mathsf k_u(X_u,\tau')\mid g]$ is continuous on $[0,1]$, by dominated convergence. Let $\omega_g$ denote its modulus of continuity. By Lemma~\ref{l.selected.pair}, $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}[b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\mid J=j]=\mathbb E[\langle b\rangle^{(n)}_{r_{j+1}}\mid g]$, so that, for $u\in[r_j,r_{j+1}]$, \eqref{e.pair.uniform.n} at the mesh point $r_{j+1}$ gives
\begin{equation*}
 \Bigl|\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\bigm|J=j\bigr]-\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_u\bigm|g\bigr]\Bigr|
 \leqslant5\,\|b\|_\infty\,\delta_n(g)+\omega_g(1/n),
\end{equation*}
which proves \eqref{e.cascade.pair.uniform}. Next, since $\mathbb P^{\mathrm{cas}}(J=j)=1/n$, we have $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}[\varphi(r_J)\,b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)]=\frac1n\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\varphi(r_j)\,\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}[b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\mid J=j]$, so the previous display gives
\begin{equation*}
 \Bigl|\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[\varphi(r_J)\,b(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\bigr]
 -\frac1n\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}\varphi(r_j)\,\mathbb E\bigl[\langle b\rangle_{r_j}\bigm|g\bigr]\Bigr|
 \leqslant\|\varphi\|_\infty\bigl(5\|b\|_\infty\delta_n(g)+\omega_g(1/n)\bigr),
\end{equation*}
and the Riemann sum converges to the right side of \eqref{e.cascade.pair.integrated}, the integrand being continuous. Finally, all the quantities in \eqref{e.cascade.pair.uniform} and \eqref{e.cascade.pair.integrated} are bounded by $\|\varphi\|_\infty\|b\|_\infty$ (with $\varphi\equiv1$ in the first case), so the statements averaged over $g$ follow by dominated convergence.

\label{r.pair.derivatives}
For the cascade with $n-1$ levels at the step path $\overline q^{(n)}$, a Gaussian integration by parts gives, as for \eqref{e.cascade.lipschitz}, $\partial_t\overline F^{\mathrm{cas}}_{N,\Omega}(t,\overline q^{(n)})=\mathbb E\,\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}[\xi(R_N(\sigma^1,\sigma^2))]$: the derivatives of the cascade free energy are expectations of two-replica observables under the Gibbs measure, with no conditioning on the separation level. Taking $b=\xi(R_N)$ and $\varphi\equiv1$ in \eqref{e.cascade.pair.integrated}, we see that the limit of this expression is $\int_0^1\mathbb E\langle\xi(R_N)\rangle_r\,\mathrm d r=\partial_t\overline F_{N,\Omega}(t,q)$. Similarly, taking $b=R_{N,s}$ in \eqref{e.cascade.pair.uniform}, we obtain, for every $r\in[0,1]$ and every species $s$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.p.conditioned}
 p_{N,s}(r)=\mathbb E\langle R_{N,s}\rangle_r
 =\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb E\,\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\bigl[R_{N,s}(\sigma^1,\sigma^2)\bigm|J=\min(\lfloor nr\rfloor,n-1)\bigr].
\end{equation}
In words, the quantity $p_N(r)$ is the limit of the mean overlap of two cascade replicas conditioned on separating at level $r$. Theorem~\ref{t.derivatives} thus disintegrates the derivative formulas of the cascade, in which the separation level is uniformly distributed, according to the value of this level.

\label{p.cascade}
For every $t\geqslant0$ and $q\in\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$, we have
\begin{equation*}
 \overline F_{N,\Omega}(t,q)=\overline F^{\mathrm{cas}}_{N,\Omega}(t,q).
\end{equation*}

Let $q$ be regular, and let $\overline q^{(n)}$ be as in Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}. Averaging Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(1) over~$g$, and since $q_n=q(1)$, we obtain
\begin{equation*}
 \overline F^{\mathrm{cas}}_{N,\Omega}(t,\overline q^{(n)})
 =t\xi(\lambda_N)+\lambda_N\cdot q(1)-\frac1N\mathbb E\,U^{(n)}(0,X_0;g).
\end{equation*}
As $n\to\infty$, the right side converges to $\overline F_{N,\Omega}(t,q)$ by Lemma~\ref{l.frozen.convergence} and \eqref{e.free.energy.V}. On the left side, $0\leqslant\overline q^{(n)}-q\leqslant q(r_{j+1})-q(r_j)$ on $[r_j,r_{j+1})$, so that $\|\overline q^{(n)}_s-q_s\|_{L^1}\leqslant n^{-1}(q_s(1)-q_s(0))$ for every species $s$, and the left side converges to $\overline F^{\mathrm{cas}}_{N,\Omega}(t,q)$ by \eqref{e.cascade.lipschitz}. This proves the proposition for regular paths. The general case follows from the density of regular paths in $\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$, by Proposition~\ref{p.extension} and \eqref{e.cascade.lipschitz}. The identity concerns the corrected free energies as a whole: the terminal value $q(1)$ alone is not continuous in $L^1$, and it may be infinite for a general path in $\mathcal Q_1^{\mathscr S}$.

\label{r.entropy}
For the finite-level cascade of Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}, the relative entropy of the Gibbs weights \eqref{e.cascade.gibbs} with respect to the cascade weights is
\begin{equation}\label{e.gibbs.variational}
 \operatorname{Ent}(\widetilde{\mathsf v}\mid\mathsf v):=\sum_\alpha\widetilde{\mathsf v}_\alpha\log\frac{\widetilde{\mathsf v}_\alpha}{\mathsf v_\alpha}
 =\sum_\alpha\widetilde{\mathsf v}_\alpha U(1,x_\alpha;g)-\log\sum_\alpha\mathsf v_\alpha e^{U(1,x_\alpha;g)}.
\end{equation}

Under $\mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}$, the first term on the right of \eqref{e.gibbs.variational} has mean $\mathbb E[U(1,X^{(n)}_1;g)\mid g]$ by Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(2), and the second has mean $\mathbb E[U^{(n)}(0,X_0;g)\mid g]$ by Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(1). Applying It\^o's formula to the frozen problem as in the proof of Lemma~\ref{l.ito}, we obtain, for every realization of $g$,
\begin{equation}\label{e.leaf.entropy}
\begin{split}
 \mathbb E^{\mathrm{cas}}\operatorname{Ent}(\widetilde{\mathsf v}\mid\mathsf v)
 &=\mathbb E\bigl[U(1,X^{(n)}_1;g)-U^{(n)}(0,X_0;g)\bigm|g\bigr]\\
 &=\mathbb E\left[\int_0^1\rho_n(r)\,\nabla U^{(n)}\cdot D(r)\nabla U^{(n)}(r,X^{(n)}_r;g)\,\mathrm d r\Bigm|g\right],
\end{split}
\end{equation}
all terms being integrable, since $U(1,\cdot\,;g)$ has linear growth and $X^{(n)}_1$ has Gaussian tails. The right side of \eqref{e.leaf.entropy} is the cost $A_q$ of Definition~\ref{d.free.energy} for the frozen branch. As $n\to\infty$, it converges to $\mathbb E[A_q\mid g]=\mathbb E[U(1,X_1;g)-U(0,X_0;g)\mid g]$, by \eqref{e.ito.identity}: the second term in \eqref{e.leaf.entropy} converges by Lemma~\ref{l.frozen.convergence}, and the first by Lemma~\ref{l.selected.leaf}(2) and Proposition~\ref{p.selected.limit}, since $|U(1,x;g)|\leqslant|U(1,0;g)|+\sqrt N|x|$ and the second moments of $X^{(n)}_1$ are bounded uniformly in $n$, the drift of the frozen branch being bounded by $2\sqrt N\|\dot q\|_\infty$. Thus the cost is, in expectation, the limit of the relative entropy of the Gibbs weights of the leaves with respect to the cascade weights.
